window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"index.html","page":"The System Design Field Guide — distributed systems & HLD, made visual","section":"System design, made visual.","tool":"home","text":"System design, made visual. The famous case studies — URL shortener, rate limiter, news feed, chat, Uber, YouTube, payments, even LLM serving — plus the core concepts behind them. Every idea is explained twice : once in plain English with an everyday analogy, then precisely the way you'd say it to a","keywords":["system","design","made","visual"]},{"url":"fundamentals/index.html","page":"Reading a design question","section":"Reading a system-design question.","tool":"fundamentals","text":"Reading a system-design question. A system-design interview isn't a quiz with one right answer — it's a 35-minute conversation where you turn a vague prompt (\"design Twitter\") into a sensible architecture, out loud, while explaining your trade-offs. This page is the framework every case study in thi","keywords":["reading","system","design","question"]},{"url":"fundamentals/index.html#why","page":"Reading a design question","section":"What's really being tested","tool":"fundamentals","text":"01 What's really being tested The interviewer doesn't expect you to have memorised the real Twitter. They want to see how you think : can you scope an ambiguous problem, reason about scale with numbers, make a decision and defend it, and notice what breaks when traffic grows 100×? In plain English a","keywords":["what","really","being","tested"]},{"url":"fundamentals/index.html#framework","page":"Reading a design question","section":"The framework — seven moves","tool":"fundamentals","text":"02 The framework — seven moves Every design in this guide walks the same path. It keeps you calm and makes sure you never forget the boring-but-critical steps (like estimating scale) that separate strong candidates from the rest. 1 Clarify & scope Ask questions. Nail the few features that matter, an","keywords":["the","framework","seven","moves"]},{"url":"fundamentals/index.html#requirements","page":"Reading a design question","section":"Functional vs non-functional","tool":"fundamentals","text":"03 Functional vs non-functional The single most useful split. Functional requirements are features. Non-functional requirements are the qualities — and they are what actually shape a distributed system. ✅ Functional (what it does) \"A user can post a tweet.\" \"A user sees a feed of people they follow.","keywords":["functional","vs","non"]},{"url":"fundamentals/index.html#numbers","page":"Reading a design question","section":"Always anchor on scale","tool":"fundamentals","text":"04 Always anchor on scale \"Design Twitter\" for 100 users is a weekend project; for 300 million it's a different machine entirely. Before drawing boxes, say the numbers out loud: daily active users, reads per second, writes per second, storage per year. Those numbers justify every later decision — \"w","keywords":["always","anchor","on","scale"]},{"url":"fundamentals/estimation.html","page":"Back-of-the-envelope estimation","section":"Back-of-the-envelope math.","tool":"fundamentals","text":"Back-of-the-envelope math. Estimation is the step most people skip and interviewers most want to see. It's not about precision — it's about turning \"design YouTube\" into \"≈ 1 million writes a day, 100 : 1 reads, petabytes a year\" so your architecture is grounded in reality. The good news: it's three","keywords":["back","of","the","envelope","math"]},{"url":"fundamentals/estimation.html#why","page":"Back-of-the-envelope estimation","section":"Why bother","tool":"fundamentals","text":"01 Why bother Numbers settle arguments. \"Should we shard the database?\" is unanswerable until you know it must hold 50 TB and one machine holds 2 TB — then the answer is obviously yes, and you can say why . Estimation converts hand-waving into decisions. In plain English analogy It's the same as a b","keywords":["why","bother"]},{"url":"fundamentals/estimation.html#recipe","page":"Back-of-the-envelope estimation","section":"The recipe — three formulas","tool":"fundamentals","text":"02 The recipe — three formulas Start from one number you can guess or are told — daily active users (DAU) — and a couple of assumptions. Everything else follows. Writes per second DAU × (writes per user per day) ÷ 86,400. There are ~86,400 seconds in a day — round to 10⁵ to do it in your head. Reads","keywords":["the","recipe","three","formulas"]},{"url":"fundamentals/estimation.html#calculator","page":"Back-of-the-envelope estimation","section":"Try it — a live capacity calculator","tool":"fundamentals","text":"03 Try it — a live capacity calculator Drag the sliders or pick a preset. Watch QPS, storage, and the implied server count update, with the formula shown next to each number. This same calculator appears on the case studies, pre-loaded for each system. These are order-of-magnitude figures — the poin","keywords":["try","it","live","capacity","calculator"]},{"url":"fundamentals/estimation.html#rounding","page":"Back-of-the-envelope estimation","section":"Round ruthlessly — powers of two & ten","tool":"fundamentals","text":"04 Round ruthlessly — powers of two & ten Interviewers want speed, not decimals. Round every number to one significant figure and lean on these: Quantity Round to Why it helps Seconds in a day 10⁵ (really 86,400) Convert per-day to per-second instantly. 2¹⁰ 1 thousand (1,024) KB ≈ 10³ B, MB ≈ 10⁶, G","keywords":["round","ruthlessly","powers","of","two","ten"]},{"url":"fundamentals/numbers.html","page":"Numbers you must know","section":"Numbers you must know.","tool":"fundamentals","text":"Numbers you must know. A handful of latency and size numbers let you answer \"is that fast?\" and \"will that fit?\" on instinct. You don't need them to the nanosecond — you need the relative scale: memory is ~100× faster than SSD, which is ~100× faster than a cross-country network round trip. Internali","keywords":["numbers","you","must","know"]},{"url":"fundamentals/numbers.html#latency","page":"Numbers you must know","section":"Latency numbers every engineer should know","tool":"fundamentals","text":"01 Latency numbers every engineer should know The classic table (rounded to 2026 hardware). The story it tells: stay in memory if you can, batch your network calls, and never hit disk on the hot path. Operation Time In human terms L1 cache reference ~1 ns 1 second Branch mispredict ~3 ns 3 seconds M","keywords":["latency","numbers","every","engineer","should","know"]},{"url":"fundamentals/numbers.html#powers","page":"Numbers you must know","section":"Powers of two → data sizes","tool":"fundamentals","text":"02 Powers of two → data sizes Storage is counted in powers of two, but for estimation we round to powers of ten. Know the ladder so \"16-byte IDs × 1 billion rows\" lands as \"~16 GB\" without a calculator. Power Exact Approx Name 2¹⁰ 1,024 ~1 thousand Kilobyte (KB) 2²⁰ 1,048,576 ~1 million Megabyte (MB","keywords":["powers","of","two","data","sizes"]},{"url":"fundamentals/numbers.html#rules","page":"Numbers you must know","section":"Rules of thumb that fall out","tool":"fundamentals","text":"03 Rules of thumb that fall out ⚡ Memory is ~100,000× faster than a network hop So a cache hit (~1 ms) vs a cross-region call (~100 ms) is the difference between instant and sluggish. Cache aggressively. 🌍 Speed of light sets a floor You can't beat ~30 ms across a continent or ~150 ms around the wo","keywords":["rules","of","thumb","that","fall","out"]},{"url":"fundamentals/numbers.html#percentiles","page":"Numbers you must know","section":"Percentiles: p50, p95, p99 (tail latency)","tool":"fundamentals","text":"04 Percentiles: p50, p95, p99 (tail latency) Latency isn't one number — it's a distribution, so we quote percentiles . The \"p\" just means \"percentile\" and the number is the cutoff: p99 = 99% of requests are faster than this value; the slowest 1% are worse. So p50 is the median (half are faster), p95","keywords":["percentiles","p50","p95","p99","tail","latency"]},{"url":"fundamentals/availability.html","page":"Availability, reliability & SLAs","section":"Availability, reliability & SLAs.","tool":"fundamentals","text":"Availability, reliability & SLAs. Availability is the percentage of time your system is up and answering. It's quoted in \"nines\" — 99.9% is three nines — and each extra nine is exponentially harder and more expensive. Knowing what the nines cost (in downtime and in engineering) lets you pick the rig","keywords":["availability","reliability","slas"]},{"url":"fundamentals/availability.html#nines","page":"Availability, reliability & SLAs","section":"The nines","tool":"fundamentals","text":"01 The nines The headline table. The jump from three nines to four isn't 10× more effort by accident — it usually means removing every manual step from recovery, because there's no time for a human to react. Availability Name Downtime / year Downtime / day 90% one nine 36.5 days 2.4 hours 99% two ni","keywords":["the","nines"]},{"url":"fundamentals/availability.html#how","page":"Availability, reliability & SLAs","section":"How you buy availability: redundancy","tool":"fundamentals","text":"02 How you buy availability: redundancy The core trick is simple: have more than one of everything, and remove single points of failure (SPOFs). If any one component going down can take the system with it, that's your ceiling. 🧱 Redundancy Multiple servers behind a load balancer, replica databases,","keywords":["how","you","buy","availability","redundancy"]},{"url":"fundamentals/availability.html#slas","page":"Availability, reliability & SLAs","section":"SLI, SLO & SLA","tool":"fundamentals","text":"03 SLI, SLO & SLA Three terms that get muddled. They nest : you measure an SLI, target it with an SLO, and promise a looser version in an SLA. SLI — Indicator (the measurement) A number you actually observe about the service's health, from the user's perspective . \"99.95% of requests succeeded in < ","keywords":["sli","slo","sla"]},{"url":"fundamentals/availability.html#error-budget","page":"Availability, reliability & SLAs","section":"Error budgets","tool":"fundamentals","text":"04 Error budgets An SLO below 100% is permission to fail a little. The leftover — 100% − SLO — is your error budget : a concrete amount of \"badness\" (downtime, slow or failed requests) you're allowed to spend each window. Flip the nines into a monthly allowance: SLO Error budget Allowed \"bad\" time /","keywords":["error","budgets"]},{"url":"fundamentals/consistency.html","page":"Consistency, CAP & PACELC","section":"Consistency, CAP & PACELC.","tool":"fundamentals","text":"Consistency, CAP & PACELC. When data lives on many machines, \"what's the current value?\" stops being obvious — different replicas can briefly disagree. Consistency is the promise about how fresh a read is. CAP and its 2026-standard refinement PACELC tell you the unavoidable trade-offs. This is the c","keywords":["consistency","cap","pacelc"]},{"url":"fundamentals/consistency.html#models","page":"Consistency, CAP & PACELC","section":"Strong vs eventual consistency","tool":"fundamentals","text":"01 Strong vs eventual consistency Two ends of a spectrum: 🎯 Strong consistency Every read returns the most recent write, always. Simple to reason about, but requires coordination between replicas — which costs latency and availability. 🌊 Eventual consistency Replicas may briefly disagree, but conv","keywords":["strong","vs","eventual","consistency"]},{"url":"fundamentals/consistency.html#cap","page":"Consistency, CAP & PACELC","section":"The CAP theorem","tool":"fundamentals","text":"02 The CAP theorem CAP says: when a network partition happens (some machines can't talk to others — and at scale, it will ), you must choose between two of three: C — Consistency Every read sees the latest write (or an error). A — Availability Every request gets a non-error response (possibly stale)","keywords":["the","cap","theorem"]},{"url":"fundamentals/consistency.html#pacelc","page":"Consistency, CAP & PACELC","section":"PACELC — the part interviewers want in 2026","tool":"fundamentals","text":"03 PACELC — the part interviewers want in 2026 CAP only describes behaviour during a partition — which is rare. PACELC adds the everyday case: \"if Partition, then A or C; Else (normal operation), Latency or Consistency.\" Even with a healthy network, keeping replicas in lockstep costs latency. So the","keywords":["pacelc","the","part","interviewers","want","in"]},{"url":"blocks/index.html","page":"The building blocks","section":"The boxes you draw.","tool":"blocks","text":"The boxes you draw. Almost every architecture is assembled from the same small kit of parts. Learn what each box does and when to reach for it, and \"design a scalable backend\" becomes a matter of arranging familiar pieces. This module is that kit; the rest of the guide just combines them.","keywords":["the","boxes","you","draw"]},{"url":"blocks/index.html#path","page":"The building blocks","section":"The path of a request","tool":"blocks","text":"01 The path of a request In plain English analogy It's a LEGO set. There's a small box of standard bricks — a baseplate, beams, connectors — and almost anything you build is those same pieces arranged differently. Learn what each brick does once, and \"design a scalable backend\" stops being scary: it","keywords":["the","path","of","request"]},{"url":"blocks/index.html#kit","page":"The building blocks","section":"The kit, box by box","tool":"blocks","text":"02 The kit, box by box Each gets its own lesson. As a map: Load balancer Spreads incoming traffic across many identical servers, and routes around dead ones. The reason you can add capacity by adding boxes. API gateway / reverse proxy The front door: TLS, auth, rate limiting, routing to the right se","keywords":["the","kit","box","by"]},{"url":"blocks/load-balancing.html","page":"Load balancing","section":"Load balancing.","tool":"blocks","text":"Load balancing. A load balancer sits in front of a pool of identical servers and spreads requests across them — so no single server is overwhelmed, and a dead one is quietly skipped. It's the component that turns \"add more servers\" into actual extra capacity, and it's the first box you draw on almos","keywords":["load","balancing"]},{"url":"blocks/load-balancing.html#idea","page":"Load balancing","section":"The idea","tool":"blocks","text":"01 The idea In plain English analogy It's the host at a busy restaurant directing arrivals to whichever waiter is free. Diners (requests) don't pick a waiter; the host (load balancer) does — keeping everyone evenly busy and steering people away from a waiter who's gone home (a failed server). Watch ","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"blocks/load-balancing.html#l4l7","page":"Load balancing","section":"Layer 4 vs Layer 7","tool":"blocks","text":"02 Layer 4 vs Layer 7 Two flavours, named after the OSI layer they understand: 🔌 L4 — transport Routes by IP + port, blind to content. Extremely fast and cheap; can't make decisions based on the URL or headers. Good for raw TCP/UDP throughput. 🧠 L7 — application Understands HTTP: can route by path","keywords":["layer","vs"]},{"url":"blocks/load-balancing.html#algos","page":"Load balancing","section":"Algorithms & health checks","tool":"blocks","text":"03 Algorithms & health checks Round robin Next server in rotation. Simple, great when servers are equal and requests are uniform. Least connections Send to the server with the fewest in-flight requests. Better when request durations vary. Weighted Bigger servers get a larger share. Handy with mixed ","keywords":["algorithms","health","checks"]},{"url":"blocks/api-gateway.html","page":"API gateway & reverse proxies","section":"API gateway & reverse proxies.","tool":"blocks","text":"API gateway & reverse proxies. A reverse proxy sits in front of your services and speaks to the outside world on their behalf. An API gateway is a reverse proxy with opinions — it handles the cross-cutting concerns every service would otherwise re-implement: TLS, authentication, rate limiting, routi","keywords":["api","gateway","reverse","proxies"]},{"url":"blocks/api-gateway.html#idea","page":"API gateway & reverse proxies","section":"Why a front door","tool":"blocks","text":"01 Why a front door In plain English analogy It's the reception desk of an office building. Visitors don't wander straight to people's desks — they check in at reception, which verifies their ID, hands them a badge, and points them to the right floor. Your services stay focused on their work; recept","keywords":["why","front","door"]},{"url":"blocks/api-gateway.html#jobs","page":"API gateway & reverse proxies","section":"What it takes off your services' plate","tool":"blocks","text":"02 What it takes off your services' plate 🔒 TLS termination & auth Decrypt HTTPS once; verify the caller's identity (token/JWT) once. Services behind it can trust the request. 🚦 Rate limiting & throttling Protect everything downstream from abuse and spikes in one place. (See the rate limiter case ","keywords":["what","it","takes","off","your","services","plate"]},{"url":"blocks/api-gateway.html#proxy","page":"API gateway & reverse proxies","section":"Reverse proxy vs forward proxy vs load balancer","tool":"blocks","text":"03 Reverse proxy vs forward proxy vs load balancer Easy to confuse: Reverse proxy Sits in front of servers , facing clients. Clients think they're talking to one server. (Nginx, Envoy.) An API gateway is a feature-rich reverse proxy. Forward proxy Sits in front of clients , facing the internet. Hide","keywords":["reverse","proxy","vs","forward","load","balancer"]},{"url":"blocks/cdn.html","page":"CDN & the edge","section":"CDN & the edge.","tool":"blocks","text":"CDN & the edge. A Content Delivery Network is a fleet of cache servers spread across the globe. When a user requests an image, video, or script, it's served from an edge location physically near them instead of your origin server an ocean away. Since light itself takes ~150 ms to cross the planet, m","keywords":["cdn","the","edge"]},{"url":"blocks/cdn.html#idea","page":"CDN & the edge","section":"Why distance is the enemy","tool":"blocks","text":"01 Why distance is the enemy In plain English analogy Instead of one central warehouse shipping every order across the country, a CDN is like having a local depot in every city. Popular items sit in the depot near you, so delivery is minutes not days. The first person in a city to order something tr","keywords":["why","distance","is","the","enemy"]},{"url":"blocks/cdn.html#what","page":"CDN & the edge","section":"What to put on a CDN","tool":"blocks","text":"02 What to put on a CDN 🖼️ Static assets Images, CSS, JS bundles, fonts, and especially video — the bulk of most apps' bandwidth. The obvious win. ⚡ Dynamic acceleration & edge compute Modern CDNs cache personalised fragments, run code at the edge (auth, A/B tests, redirects), and keep persistent c","keywords":["what","to","put","on","cdn"]},{"url":"blocks/cdn.html#gotchas","page":"CDN & the edge","section":"Cache hit ratio is the metric","tool":"blocks","text":"03 Cache hit ratio is the metric A CDN only helps to the extent it serves from cache. A 95% hit ratio means only 1 in 20 requests bothers your origin — that's the difference between a small origin fleet and a giant one. You raise it by caching aggressively, normalising URLs, and not accidentally mar","keywords":["cache","hit","ratio","is","the","metric"]},{"url":"blocks/caching.html","page":"Caching","section":"Caching.","tool":"blocks","text":"Caching. A cache is a small, fast store (usually in-memory, like Redis) that holds copies of hot data so you don't recompute or re-fetch it. It's the single biggest scaling lever in most systems: a good cache turns a 10 ms database read into a 1 ms lookup and shields your database from the bulk of t","keywords":["caching"]},{"url":"blocks/caching.html#aside","page":"Caching","section":"The default: cache-aside","tool":"blocks","text":"01 The default: cache-aside The most common pattern. The application talks to the cache first and falls back to the database, filling the cache on a miss. Step through a read: Cache-aside read (miss → fill → hit) ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× In plain English analogy It's keeping a few favourite books on","keywords":["the","default","cache","aside"]},{"url":"blocks/caching.html#writes","page":"Caching","section":"Write strategies — pick your trade-off","tool":"blocks","text":"02 Write strategies — pick your trade-off Reads are easy; the interesting choices are about writes — how the cache and database stay in step. Switch between the three and watch the costs move:","keywords":["write","strategies","pick","your","trade","off"]},{"url":"blocks/caching.html#evict","page":"Caching","section":"Eviction, TTLs & the hard parts","tool":"blocks","text":"03 Eviction, TTLs & the hard parts A cache is small, so it must throw things out. LRU (least-recently-used) is the default; LFU (least-frequently-used) suits skewed popularity. A TTL caps how stale data can get. Two classic failure modes to name in interviews: 🔥 Hot key One key (a celebrity, a vira","keywords":["eviction","ttls","the","hard","parts"]},{"url":"blocks/messaging.html","page":"Queues, pub/sub & streams","section":"Queues, pub/sub & streams.","tool":"blocks","text":"Queues, pub/sub & streams. A message queue lets one part of your system hand work to another asynchronously — the sender drops a message and moves on; a worker picks it up later. This one idea buys you decoupling, spike absorption, and resilience. It's how you keep the user's request fast while the ","keywords":["queues","pub","sub","streams"]},{"url":"blocks/messaging.html#idea","page":"Queues, pub/sub & streams","section":"Producers, queues, consumers","tool":"blocks","text":"01 Producers, queues, consumers In plain English analogy It's the order rail in a kitchen. The waiter (producer) clips a ticket to the rail (queue) and immediately goes back to serving — they don't stand and watch it cook. Whichever chef (consumer) is free grabs the next ticket. If a rush hits, tick","keywords":["producers","queues","consumers"]},{"url":"blocks/messaging.html#patterns","page":"Queues, pub/sub & streams","section":"Queue vs pub/sub vs stream","tool":"blocks","text":"02 Queue vs pub/sub vs stream Work queue Each message is processed by one consumer from a pool — work is shared out. (RabbitMQ, SQS.) Use for tasks: send email, resize image, charge card. Pub/sub Each message is delivered to every subscriber. (SNS, Redis pub/sub.) Use for fan-out: one event, many in","keywords":["queue","vs","pub","sub","stream"]},{"url":"blocks/messaging.html#guarantees","page":"Queues, pub/sub & streams","section":"Delivery guarantees & idempotency","tool":"blocks","text":"03 Delivery guarantees & idempotency Distributed messaging can't promise perfection. You choose: 1️⃣ At-most-once May drop a message, never duplicates. Fine for telemetry where a lost sample doesn't matter. 🔁 At-least-once (the common default) Never drops, but may deliver twice (e.g., a worker cras","keywords":["delivery","guarantees","idempotency"]},{"url":"data/index.html","page":"SQL vs NoSQL","section":"SQL vs NoSQL.","tool":"data","text":"SQL vs NoSQL. The database is the heart of most designs, and \"SQL or NoSQL?\" is the question you'll answer on every one. The honest answer is \"it depends on the access pattern\" — so this page gives you a way to decide rather than a dogma. Relational databases trade some scale for powerful queries an","keywords":["sql","vs","nosql"]},{"url":"data/index.html#families","page":"SQL vs NoSQL","section":"The families","tool":"data","text":"01 The families Relational (SQL) Tables with a fixed schema, joins, and ACID transactions. PostgreSQL, MySQL. The default until you have a specific reason to leave. Key-value A giant hash map: get/put by key, nothing else. Redis, DynamoDB. Insanely fast and scalable for simple lookups. Document JSON","keywords":["the","families"]},{"url":"data/index.html#compare","page":"SQL vs NoSQL","section":"The trade-off, made concrete","tool":"data","text":"02 The trade-off, made concrete No family wins on every axis. Flip between them and watch what you gain and give up:","keywords":["the","trade","off","made","concrete"]},{"url":"data/index.html#decide","page":"SQL vs NoSQL","section":"How to decide in an interview","tool":"data","text":"03 How to decide in an interview Don't recite \"NoSQL scales better.\" Reason from the data: Relationships & transactions? (money, orders, \"must be consistent\") → SQL. Modern Postgres scales further than people think. Known, simple access pattern at huge scale? (feed by user, messages by chat) → wide-","keywords":["how","to","decide","in","an","interview"]},{"url":"data/indexing.html","page":"Indexing","section":"Indexing.","tool":"data","text":"Indexing. An index is a separate, sorted data structure that lets the database jump to the rows you want instead of scanning every one. It's the difference between flipping to a word in a book's index and reading the whole book. Indexes make reads fast — but each one slows writes and costs storage, ","keywords":["indexing"]},{"url":"data/indexing.html#why","page":"Indexing","section":"Full scan vs lookup","tool":"data","text":"01 Full scan vs lookup In plain English analogy Finding every mention of \"caching\" in a 500-page book: without an index you read all 500 pages (a full table scan). With the index at the back, you flip to \"caching → p. 142, 287\" and go straight there. The book's index is sorted alphabetically so you ","keywords":["full","scan","vs","lookup","Slow","on","big","table","scans","every","row","SELECT","FROM","users","WHERE","email","ada","com","Create","an","index","once","lookups","become","log","CREATE","INDEX","idx_users_email","ON","Composite","order","matters","this","serves","user_id","ORDER","BY","created_at","DESC","idx_posts_user_time"]},{"url":"data/indexing.html#how","page":"Indexing","section":"How indexes work — B-trees & friends","tool":"data","text":"02 How indexes work — B-trees & friends B-tree (the default) A balanced, sorted tree. Great for equality and range queries ( > , < , BETWEEN , ORDER BY ). What most relational indexes use. LSM-tree The B-tree's write-optimised mirror image: buffer writes in memory, flush them to immutable sorted fil","keywords":["how","indexes","work","trees","friends"]},{"url":"data/indexing.html#cost","page":"Indexing","section":"The cost of an index","tool":"data","text":"03 The cost of an index Indexes aren't free, and naming the cost is what separates a thoughtful answer from a reflexive \"add an index\": ✍️ Slower writes Every INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE must update every index on the table. Ten indexes = ten extra little writes per row change. 💾 More storage Indexes ","keywords":["the","cost","of","an","index"]},{"url":"data/sharding.html","page":"Sharding & partitioning","section":"Sharding & partitioning.","tool":"data","text":"Sharding & partitioning. When one database can't hold all your data or handle all your writes, you split it across many machines — each holding a shard (a slice of the rows). Done well, you scale writes and storage almost linearly. Done badly, you create a \"hot shard\" that takes all the traffic, or ","keywords":["sharding","partitioning"]},{"url":"data/sharding.html#idea","page":"Sharding & partitioning","section":"Splitting the data","tool":"data","text":"01 Splitting the data In plain English analogy One librarian can't manage a billion books. So you open several branches and split the collection by a rule — \"A–F here, G–M there.\" The rule is the shard key. A good rule keeps every branch equally busy; a bad rule sends everyone to the branch holding ","keywords":["splitting","the","data"]},{"url":"data/sharding.html#how","page":"Sharding & partitioning","section":"How to split — and the trade-off","tool":"data","text":"02 How to split — and the trade-off The reason plain hash % N is dangerous is the resharding cost — which is exactly the problem consistent hashing solves, next.","keywords":["how","to","split","and","the","trade","off"]},{"url":"data/sharding.html#pitfalls","page":"Sharding & partitioning","section":"Hot shards & cross-shard pain","tool":"data","text":"03 Hot shards & cross-shard pain 🔥 Hot shard / celebrity key If your shard key is skewed (e.g., shard by country and 60% are in one), that shard melts. Pick a high-cardinality, evenly-distributed key — or split the hot key further. 🔗 Cross-shard queries & joins A query spanning shards must hit the","keywords":["hot","shards","cross","shard","pain"]},{"url":"data/replication.html","page":"Replication & quorums","section":"Replication & quorums.","tool":"data","text":"Replication & quorums. Replication keeps copies of your data on multiple machines. It buys you two things: durability (one machine dies, the data survives) and read scaling (spread reads across copies). The catch is keeping the copies in step — and the choices you make there (synchronous vs asynchro","keywords":["replication","quorums"]},{"url":"data/replication.html#leader","page":"Replication & quorums","section":"Leader–follower replication","tool":"data","text":"01 Leader–follower replication In plain English analogy One person keeps the master notebook (the leader); a few others keep photocopies (followers) they update from it. Everyone writes to the master, but anyone can read from a copy. If the master is lost, a copy is promoted to be the new master. Th","keywords":["leader","follower","replication"]},{"url":"data/replication.html#syncasync","page":"Replication & quorums","section":"Synchronous vs asynchronous","tool":"data","text":"02 Synchronous vs asynchronous Does the leader wait for replicas to confirm before telling the client \"done\"? That single question is the durability-vs-speed dial:","keywords":["synchronous","vs","asynchronous"]},{"url":"data/replication.html#quorum","page":"Replication & quorums","section":"Leaderless & quorums","tool":"data","text":"03 Leaderless & quorums Some stores (Dynamo, Cassandra) drop the single leader entirely: the client writes to several replicas and reads from several, using a quorum to stay consistent. With N replicas, a write needs W acks and a read queries R of them. If W + R > N , any read overlaps at least one ","keywords":["leaderless","quorums"]},{"url":"data/consensus.html","page":"Consensus & leader election","section":"Consensus & leader election.","tool":"data","text":"Consensus & leader election. Once data lives on many machines, they have to agree on things — who's the leader, what order writes happened in, whether a value is committed. Consensus algorithms (Raft, Paxos) are how a cluster reaches agreement even when machines crash and networks drop messages. You","keywords":["consensus","leader","election"]},{"url":"data/consensus.html#why","page":"Consensus & leader election","section":"The problem: split-brain","tool":"data","text":"01 The problem: split-brain In plain English analogy Picture a committee that can only act through one chairperson. If a network glitch splits the committee into two rooms and each elects its own chair, you now have two people both issuing orders — and the org tears itself apart making contradictory","keywords":["the","problem","split","brain"]},{"url":"data/consensus.html#election","page":"Consensus & leader election","section":"Leader election (the Raft intuition)","tool":"data","text":"02 Leader election (the Raft intuition) Raft makes consensus approachable. Nodes are followers until one's randomised timeout fires; it becomes a candidate , asks everyone for a vote, and if it wins a majority it becomes leader and sends heartbeats to keep the role. Watch it: A candidate wins a majo","keywords":["leader","election","the","raft","intuition"]},{"url":"data/consensus.html#quorum","page":"Consensus & leader election","section":"Why a majority — and odd numbers","tool":"data","text":"03 Why a majority — and odd numbers The magic ingredient is the majority quorum : an action needs agreement from more than half the nodes. Two majorities can't exist at once, so two leaders can't be elected — split-brain is mathematically prevented. It also means the cluster keeps working as long as","keywords":["why","majority","and","odd","numbers"]},{"url":"data/consensus.html#uses","page":"Consensus & leader election","section":"Where it shows up & distributed locks","tool":"data","text":"04 Where it shows up & distributed locks You rarely build consensus yourself — you lean on a coordination service that has: ZooKeeper or etcd (and increasingly Raft built into the database). They provide leader election, configuration, and distributed locks (only one holder at a time) as a service. ","keywords":["where","it","shows","up","distributed","locks"]},{"url":"data/consistent-hashing.html","page":"Consistent hashing","section":"Consistent hashing.","tool":"data","text":"Consistent hashing. When you spread keys across N nodes with hash(key) % N , changing N — adding or removing a single node — reshuffles almost every key. At scale that's catastrophic: every cache entry invalidated, terabytes moved. Consistent hashing fixes this so that adding or removing a node move","keywords":["consistent","hashing"]},{"url":"data/consistent-hashing.html#problem","page":"Consistent hashing","section":"The problem with hash % N","tool":"data","text":"01 The problem with hash % N In plain English analogy Imagine assigning students to dorms by \"ID number mod number-of-dorms.\" Build one new dorm and suddenly almost everyone has to move rooms, because every remainder changed. Consistent hashing is a smarter rule where opening a new dorm only makes t","keywords":["the","problem","with","hash"]},{"url":"data/consistent-hashing.html#ring","page":"Consistent hashing","section":"The ring","tool":"data","text":"02 The ring Hash both servers and keys onto the same circle (say 0…2³²). A key is owned by the first server clockwise from it. Now adding a server only steals the keys in the arc just before it — everyone else is untouched. Step through adding a node: Add a server → only its neighbour's keys move ⏮ ","keywords":["the","ring"]},{"url":"data/consistent-hashing.html#vnodes","page":"Consistent hashing","section":"Virtual nodes & the lookup","tool":"data","text":"03 Virtual nodes & the lookup One problem remains: with few servers, the arcs are uneven, so some servers own far more of the ring than others. The fix is virtual nodes — place each physical server at many points around the ring (e.g., 150 each). The load smooths out, and when a server leaves, its s","keywords":["virtual","nodes","the","lookup","import","bisect","hashlib","class","Ring","def","__init__","self","vnodes","150","ring","position","node","sorted_keys","for","in","add","_hash","key","return","int","md5","encode","hexdigest","range","many","points","per","pos","insort","get","len","first","clockwise"]},{"url":"warmup/index.html","page":"The design playbook","section":"The design playbook.","tool":"warmup","text":"The design playbook. You have ~40 minutes and a blank board. This is the choreography that keeps you in control: what to say, in what order, and how long to spend. Every case study in this guide is solved with these same moves, so by the third one it's muscle memory — and a calm, structured candidat","keywords":["the","design","playbook"]},{"url":"warmup/index.html#clock","page":"The design playbook","section":"Spend your 40 minutes wisely","tool":"warmup","text":"01 Spend your 40 minutes wisely In plain English analogy Think of a pilot's pre-flight checklist. Pilots aren't forgetful — they run the list precisely because skipping a step under pressure is how things crash. These moves are your checklist: follow them in order and you'll never freeze, never forg","keywords":["spend","your","minutes","wisely"]},{"url":"warmup/index.html#checklist","page":"The design playbook","section":"The seven moves — a checklist","tool":"warmup","text":"02 The seven moves — a checklist 1 Clarify & scope \"Who uses it? Which features matter? What scale?\" Lock a small core; name what you're cutting. 2 Requirements Functional (features) and non-functional (scale, latency, availability, consistency). Say \"read-heavy or write-heavy?\" out loud. 3 Estimate","keywords":["the","seven","moves","checklist"]},{"url":"warmup/index.html#signals","page":"The design playbook","section":"What earns (and loses) points","tool":"warmup","text":"03 What earns (and loses) points ✅ Green flags Driving the conversation, justifying choices with numbers, naming trade-offs (\"I'd accept eventual consistency here because…\"), starting simple then scaling, knowing when not to add complexity. 🚩 Red flags Silent whiteboarding, name-dropping tech with ","keywords":["what","earns","and","loses","points"]},{"url":"warmup/url-shortener.html","page":"Design a URL shortener","section":"Design a URL shortener.","tool":"warmup","text":"Design a URL shortener. Turn long.example.com/a/very/long/path into sho.rt/abc123 and redirect visitors back. The core is simple — which is exactly why it's the canonical warm-up for the full method: scope it, estimate it, design an API and store, choose a code-generation strategy, then make a massi","keywords":["design","url","shortener"]},{"url":"warmup/url-shortener.html#requirements","page":"Design a URL shortener","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"warmup","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Create a short URL from a long one (optional custom alias + expiry). Redirect a short URL to its long URL. Click analytics is a common nice-to-have. 📐 Non-functional Massively read-heavy (~100:1 to 1000:1 reads:writes). Redirects fast (< 100 ms) and highly avail","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"warmup/url-shortener.html#estimate","page":"Design a URL shortener","section":"Capacity estimation","tool":"warmup","text":"02 Capacity estimation Anchor on a realistic read-heavy model: ~100M DAU each following a few links/day → hundreds of millions of redirects/day; new-URL creation is tiny by comparison. Takeaways: only a few hundred writes/s but tens of thousands of reads/s — so optimise hard for reads (cache!) — and","keywords":["capacity","estimation"]},{"url":"warmup/url-shortener.html#api","page":"Design a URL shortener","section":"API & data model","tool":"warmup","text":"03 API & data model POST /urls { long_url, alias?, expiry? } → { short_url } GET /{short_code} → 301/302 redirect to long_url The data model is almost nothing — a single mapping with the short code as primary key: short_code Primary key, e.g. abc123 (7 chars). Looked up on every redirect. Keep a UNI","keywords":["api","data","model"]},{"url":"warmup/url-shortener.html#codes","page":"Design a URL shortener","section":"Generating short codes","tool":"warmup","text":"04 Generating short codes Mint a unique 7-char code in base62 (0-9 a-z A-Z; not base64 — + / / are URL-unsafe). 62⁷ ≈ 3.52 trillion codes; 6 chars (≈56.8B) is borderline, 7 has headroom. 🔢 Counter → base62 Encode a monotonic id in base62. Zero collisions , no existence check. But a single counter i","keywords":["generating","short","codes","ALPHABET","abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ","BASE","def","encode","num","int","str","if","loop","below","never","runs","for","return","raise","ValueError","id","must","be","non","negative","out","while","rem","divmod","append","join","reversed","remainders","come","least","significant","first"]},{"url":"warmup/url-shortener.html#architecture","page":"Design a URL shortener","section":"High-level architecture","tool":"warmup","text":"05 High-level architecture Two paths: a rare write (create) and a hot read (redirect). The redirect path carries ~99.9% of traffic and is served almost entirely from cache; click events fire async so logging never blocks the redirect. The redirect (read) path ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2×","keywords":["high","level","architecture"]},{"url":"warmup/url-shortener.html#caching","page":"Design a URL shortener","section":"Caching & 301 vs 302","tool":"warmup","text":"06 Caching & 301 vs 302 Caching is the single biggest lever. The access pattern is Zipfian — a small hot set of links gets most clicks — so a modest Redis footprint covers the working set and yields 90–95%+ hit ratios , making p99 redirects single-digit ms. 301 (permanent) Browsers/CDNs cache it, so","keywords":["caching","301","vs","302"]},{"url":"warmup/url-shortener.html#failure","page":"Design a URL shortener","section":"Failure modes & resilience","tool":"warmup","text":"07 Failure modes & resilience 🧊 Cache death / cold start The dangerous one: a flushed cache sends 100% of reads to a DB provisioned for ~5% → thundering herd . Mitigate with a replicated cache (Sentinel/Cluster failover), request coalescing / single-flight on misses, jittered TTLs to avoid synchron","keywords":["failure","modes","resilience"]},{"url":"warmup/url-shortener.html#multiregion","page":"Design a URL shortener","section":"Multi-region & the edge","tool":"warmup","text":"08 Multi-region & the edge Serve redirects close to users. The modern (2026) pattern collapses the classic \"cache + DB\" tiers into a single globally-replicated edge KV layer: Edge KV Run redirect logic at the edge against Cloudflare Workers KV or DynamoDB Global Tables — global single-digit-ms redir","keywords":["multi","region","the","edge"]},{"url":"warmup/url-shortener.html#ops","page":"Design a URL shortener","section":"Observability & cost","tool":"warmup","text":"09 Observability & cost Metrics & SLOs Redirect latency p99 (SLO < 50–100 ms at the edge); cache hit ratio (SLO ≥ 90–95% — your leading indicator, alert below ~85%); redirect availability ≥ 99.99%; create success rate ≥ 99.9%; replication lag; 404 rate (expired/invalid). Alerts Hit ratio drop (herd/","keywords":["observability","cost"]},{"url":"warmup/url-shortener.html#recap","page":"Design a URL shortener","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"warmup","text":"10 Trade-offs & interview recap Why is generating codes from a counter (or a key-generation service) usually better than hashing the long URL? Hashing is too slow to compute Hashing causes collisions (two URLs → same code), forcing check-and-retry; counters/KGS guarantee uniqueness You can't base62-","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"warmup/rate-limiter.html","page":"Design a rate limiter","section":"Design a rate limiter.","tool":"warmup","text":"Design a rate limiter. Cap how many requests a client may make in a window — to stop abuse, absorb spikes, and protect fragile downstreams. The interesting part isn't the counting; it's making one global limit hold atomically across a fleet of gateways, choosing an algorithm whose burst behaviour ma","keywords":["design","rate","limiter"]},{"url":"warmup/rate-limiter.html#requirements","page":"Design a rate limiter","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"warmup","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Given a key (user / API-key / IP), allow or reject each request against a configured limit (e.g. 1000 req/min). Return 429 with Retry-After on reject. Support per-route and per-tier limits; ideally tunable without redeploy. 📐 Non-functional Correct globally , no","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"warmup/rate-limiter.html#estimate","page":"Design a rate limiter","section":"Capacity estimation","tool":"warmup","text":"02 Capacity estimation The limiter sees every request, so its load equals total API traffic. Size the counter store's ops/s, not its storage — state per key is a few bytes. Quantity Assumption Result API traffic (peak) 1M req/s 1M limiter checks/s State per key 2 fields (tokens + ts) ≈ 64 B 10M acti","keywords":["capacity","estimation"]},{"url":"warmup/rate-limiter.html#api","page":"Design a rate limiter","section":"API & response contract","tool":"warmup","text":"03 API & response contract Internally the limiter is one call; externally it shapes the HTTP response a client sees. CALL allow(key, cost=1) → { allowed, remaining, retry_after } HTTP 429 Too Many Requests Retry-After: 30 · RateLimit: \"default\";r=0;t=30 429 + Retry-After The standard reject (RFC 658","keywords":["api","response","contract"]},{"url":"warmup/rate-limiter.html#algorithms","page":"Design a rate limiter","section":"The algorithm choice","tool":"warmup","text":"04 The algorithm choice Five algorithms span the accuracy / memory / burst trade-off. Drag through them: Default pick: token bucket when short bursts are fine (most public APIs); sliding-window counter for a smooth approximate cap with trivial memory; GCRA ( CL.THROTTLE ) for an exact rolling window","keywords":["the","algorithm","choice"]},{"url":"warmup/rate-limiter.html#distributed","page":"Design a rate limiter","section":"Making it correct across many servers","tool":"warmup","text":"05 Making it correct across many servers Traffic is spread over many gateways. Per-instance counters are wrong : a user doing 50 req on gateway A and 50 on B looks compliant on each but did 100 globally. So the counter must be shared — typically Redis — and the check must be atomic . The read-modify","keywords":["making","it","correct","across","many","servers","KEYS","bucket","key","ARGV","rate","capacity","now","seconds","cost","local","cap","tonumber","st","redis","call","HMGET","tokens","ts","last","if","nil","then","end","first","sight","start","full","elapsed","math","max","guard","backward","clocks","min"]},{"url":"warmup/rate-limiter.html#placement","page":"Design a rate limiter","section":"Where the limiter lives","tool":"warmup","text":"06 Where the limiter lives Client SDK Courtesy throttle to save bandwidth; untrusted — never the enforcement point. API gateway / edge The standard 2026 enforcement point (Kong, Cloudflare, AWS API Gateway, NGINX). Blocked traffic never touches app servers. Lacks deep business context. Sidecar / mes","keywords":["where","the","limiter","lives"]},{"url":"warmup/rate-limiter.html#failure","page":"Design a rate limiter","section":"Failure modes & multi-region","tool":"warmup","text":"07 Failure modes & multi-region 🔌 Redis down → fail-open or fail-closed? Fail-open (allow when undecidable) keeps the API up but risks flooding an unprotected backend during the outage. Fail-closed protects the backend but takes you down. Usual call: fail-open for normal APIs, fail-closed where the","keywords":["failure","modes","multi","region"]},{"url":"warmup/rate-limiter.html#ops","page":"Design a rate limiter","section":"Observability & cost","tool":"warmup","text":"08 Observability & cost Metrics Allowed vs blocked (429) rate per route/tenant; near-limit ratio (passed but close — a capacity early-warning); limiter decision latency p99; Redis error/timeout rate; per-shard ops imbalance (hot-shard detection); fail-open activation count. SLOs Added latency p99 < ","keywords":["observability","cost"]},{"url":"warmup/rate-limiter.html#recap","page":"Design a rate limiter","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"warmup","text":"09 Trade-offs & interview recap Why must the counter check run as one atomic operation rather than read-then-write? Atomic operations are faster Concurrent requests can both read the same count and both \"allow,\" over-admitting past the limit — only atomicity (Lua / CL.THROTTLE) closes the race Redis","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"warmup/unique-id.html","page":"Design a unique-ID generator","section":"Design a unique-ID generator.","tool":"warmup","text":"Design a unique-ID generator. Many systems need globally unique IDs at high volume — tweets, orders, messages — ideally time-sortable (cheap \"newest first\", index-friendly inserts) and generated without coordination (no central bottleneck). The real decision in 2026 is which scheme: Snowflake's comp","keywords":["design","unique","id","generator"]},{"url":"warmup/unique-id.html#requirements","page":"Design a unique-ID generator","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"warmup","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Produce IDs that are globally unique and roughly ordered by creation time, generated locally by any node, with no duplicates ever. 📐 Non-functional High throughput (10⁴–10⁶ IDs/s fleet-wide), no per-ID coordination on the hot path, compact enough to index well, ","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"warmup/unique-id.html#options","page":"Design a unique-ID generator","section":"The options","tool":"warmup","text":"02 The options Five schemes span the trade-off between sortability, coordination, size, and index-friendliness: Pick: UUIDv7 as the modern default when 128 bits is acceptable (native DB support makes it a one-line swap); Snowflake when you need compact 8-byte numeric IDs or a sortable shard key. Bot","keywords":["the","options"]},{"url":"warmup/unique-id.html#layout","page":"Design a unique-ID generator","section":"Snowflake — the 64-bit layout","tool":"warmup","text":"03 Snowflake — the 64-bit layout Snowflake packs four fields into a 64-bit integer; each node reads its clock, stamps its machine id, and counts within the millisecond: Snowflake — 64 bits 1 unused (sign) 41 bits timestamp (ms since epoch) 10 bits machine id 12 bits sequence Timestamp first → IDs so","keywords":["snowflake","the","bit","layout"]},{"url":"warmup/unique-id.html#code","page":"Design a unique-ID generator","section":"The generator & the clock-skew gotcha","tool":"warmup","text":"04 The generator & the clock-skew gotcha snowflake.py Copy class Snowflake: def __init__(self, machine_id: int, epoch: int = 1704067200000): self.machine_id = machine_id # 0..1023, leased per node self.epoch = epoch # custom epoch (ms) self.last_ms = -1 self.seq = 0 def next_id(self, now_ms: int) ->","keywords":["the","generator","clock","skew","gotcha","class","Snowflake","def","__init__","self","machine_id","int","epoch","leased","per","node","custom","ms","last_ms","seq","next_id","now_ms","if","CLOCK","WENT","BACKWARDS","raise","RuntimeError","moved","back","refuse","until","it","catches","up","xFFF","bit","sequence","exhausted","this"]},{"url":"warmup/unique-id.html#uuidv7","page":"Design a unique-ID generator","section":"UUIDv7 — the 2026 default","tool":"warmup","text":"05 UUIDv7 — the 2026 default UUIDv7 (RFC 9562, 2024) puts a 48-bit Unix-ms timestamp in the most-significant bits, then version/variant bits and 74 random bits. Same k-sortable, index-friendly property as Snowflake, but 128-bit, coordination-free, and standardised — and as of 2026 it's built in: uui","keywords":["uuidv7","the","default","Python","ships","uuid","uuid7","monotonic","within","ms","import","id","c3e2","time","ordered","coordination","free","PostgreSQL","it","natively","CREATE","TABLE","orders","PRIMARY","KEY","DEFAULT","The","leading","bit","timestamp","makes","inserts","land","at","right","edge","of","tree","index","locality"]},{"url":"warmup/unique-id.html#coordination","page":"Design a unique-ID generator","section":"Scaling without per-ID coordination","tool":"warmup","text":"06 Scaling without per-ID coordination Snowflake's only coordination is worker-id assignment at startup — leased from a strongly-consistent coordinator (ZooKeeper ephemeral node / etcd lease). After that, every ID is minted locally. If you instead want a monotonic counter (Flickr ticket / Meituan Le","keywords":["scaling","without","per","id","coordination"]},{"url":"warmup/unique-id.html#failure","page":"Design a unique-ID generator","section":"Failure modes & multi-region","tool":"warmup","text":"07 Failure modes & multi-region ⏰ Clock skew Backward jumps break monotonicity and risk duplicate (ts, seq) tuples. Run NTP slew-only; refuse / wait / borrow-sequence on regression. Forward jumps are harmless (skip ms). ⚠️ Worker-id collision Two nodes sharing a worker id in the same ms → duplicate ","keywords":["failure","modes","multi","region"]},{"url":"warmup/unique-id.html#ops","page":"Design a unique-ID generator","section":"Observability & cost","tool":"warmup","text":"08 Observability & cost Metrics IDs/s per node; sequence-exhaustion (spin) rate; clock-regression events (alert immediately); worker-id lease renewals/failures; coordinator availability. SLOs Zero duplicate IDs (the only hard invariant); generation latency p99 sub-microsecond (it's local arithmetic)","keywords":["observability","cost"]},{"url":"warmup/unique-id.html#recap","page":"Design a unique-ID generator","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"warmup","text":"09 Trade-offs & interview recap Why put the timestamp in the high bits of the ID rather than the low bits? It makes the number smaller So IDs sort chronologically — later IDs are numerically larger, giving cheap \"newest first\" ordering and right-edge (index-friendly) inserts The CPU requires it Righ","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"core/distributed-cache.html","page":"Design a distributed cache","section":"Design a distributed cache.","tool":"core","text":"Design a distributed cache. A single Redis box is easy. The real problem is a cache spanning hundreds of machines that stays fast as nodes join and leave, survives a node dying without stampeding the database, and copes with one wildly popular key. It pulls together consistent hashing , replication,","keywords":["design","distributed","cache"]},{"url":"core/distributed-cache.html#requirements","page":"Design a distributed cache","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"core","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional get(key) , put(key, value, ttl) , delete(key) , plus atomic ops ( incr , CAS) and batch ( mget ). Values are small blobs; any entry may be evicted. 📐 Non-functional Sub-millisecond reads, millions of ops/s, horizontally scalable, tolerant of node failure (a lost","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"core/distributed-cache.html#estimate","page":"Design a distributed cache","section":"Capacity estimation","tool":"core","text":"02 Capacity estimation Size the hit ratio (it decides DB load), memory (the working set, not the whole DB), and ops/s per node . Worked example fronting a read-heavy DB at 1M reads/s: Quantity Assumption Result DB load @ 95% hit 1M reads/s × 5% miss 50k reads/s reach the DB (20× shielding) DB load @","keywords":["capacity","estimation"]},{"url":"core/distributed-cache.html#api","page":"Design a distributed cache","section":"API & data model","tool":"core","text":"03 API & data model GET get(key) / mget(k1,k2,…) → value | null · batch collapses N round trips into 1 SET set(key, value, EX ttl) [NX|XX] TTL is first-class · NX/XX = set-if-absent/present (CAS) ATOMIC incr · getdel · WATCH/MULTI · Lua server-side atomicity for counters & leases Stored values are s","keywords":["api","data","model"]},{"url":"core/distributed-cache.html#architecture","page":"Design a distributed cache","section":"Partitioning & the read path","tool":"core","text":"04 Partitioning & the read path Data is too big for one box, so shard it with consistent hashing (or Redis Cluster's 16384 CRC16 slots — keys→slots→nodes, so resharding moves slots, not keys). A smart client owns the topology, hashes the key, and talks straight to the owning node — no proxy on the h","keywords":["partitioning","the","read","path"]},{"url":"core/distributed-cache.html#hashing","page":"Design a distributed cache","section":"Consistent hashing deep dive","tool":"core","text":"05 Consistent hashing deep dive hash(key) % N is fatal for a cache: change N and ~ N/(N+1) of keys remap — adding 1 node to 10 moves ~91% of keys to cold nodes → mass miss → DB stampede. The ring maps nodes and keys into one hash space; a key is owned by the first node clockwise, so membership chang","keywords":["consistent","hashing","deep","dive","import","bisect","hashlib","class","HashRing","def","__init__","self","nodes","vnodes","150","_keys","sorted","ring","positions","_ring","position","node","for","in","add","_h","return","int","md5","encode","hexdigest","range","scatter","many","points","per","insort","get_node","key","if"]},{"url":"core/distributed-cache.html#replication","page":"Design a distributed cache","section":"Replication & failure","tool":"core","text":"06 Replication & failure Each shard = one primary + async replicas. On primary death a coordinator (Redis Sentinel, or cluster gossip + quorum) promotes a replica in single-digit seconds ; consistent hashing confines the impact to that shard. Replication is async, so an acked write can be lost on fa","keywords":["replication","failure"]},{"url":"core/distributed-cache.html#hotkey","page":"Design a distributed cache","section":"Eviction, hot keys & the stampede","tool":"core","text":"07 Eviction, hot keys & the stampede At maxmemory , evict by LRU/LFU + TTL. Redis approximates LRU by sampling (default 5 keys) and LFU with an 8-bit decaying counter. Two scaling traps remain. First, the hot key : consistent hashing balances keys , not request rate , so one viral key melts its sing","keywords":["eviction","hot","keys","the","stampede"]},{"url":"core/distributed-cache.html#writes","page":"Design a distributed cache","section":"Write policies & consistency","tool":"core","text":"08 Write policies & consistency How writes touch the cache vs the DB is its own trade-off: Default: cache-aside + TTL (keeps the cache off the write path; TTL is a staleness bound, not a consistency mechanism). Delete, don't update on writes, and drive invalidation from the DB change log (CDC) when ","keywords":["write","policies","consistency"]},{"url":"core/distributed-cache.html#ops","page":"Design a distributed cache","section":"Multi-region, observability & cost","tool":"core","text":"09 Multi-region, observability & cost Multi-region Default to a local cache per region (low latency, blast-radius contained), filled from a regional DB; rely on TTL or a cross-region invalidation fan-out. A globally-replicated cache (Redis Enterprise Active-Active / CRDTs) is eventually consistent a","keywords":["multi","region","observability","cost"]},{"url":"core/distributed-cache.html#recap","page":"Design a distributed cache","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"core","text":"10 Trade-offs & interview recap Why is consistent hashing preferred over hash(key) % N for a cache cluster? It's faster to compute Adding/removing a node remaps only ~1/N of keys, avoiding a near-total cache wipe and a database stampede It guarantees no hot keys Right: with % N , changing N reshuffl","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"core/web-crawler.html","page":"Design a web crawler","section":"Design a web crawler.","tool":"core","text":"Design a web crawler. Systematically download the web — following links page to page — to feed a search index. The web is enormous, looping, and trap-laden, so the design is about scale and discipline : never re-fetching a URL, being polite to each host, deduping near-identical content, and spreadin","keywords":["design","web","crawler"]},{"url":"core/web-crawler.html#requirements","page":"Design a web crawler","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"core","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Start from seed URLs, download each page, extract + normalize links, store content for indexing, and re-crawl on a freshness schedule. 📐 Non-functional Billions of pages → massively parallel. Polite (one in-flight request/host, respect robots.txt ). Robust to tr","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"core/web-crawler.html#estimate","page":"Design a web crawler","section":"Capacity estimation","tool":"core","text":"02 Capacity estimation Quantity Assumption Result Crawl rate 1B pages / 30 days 1e9 ÷ (30 × 86,400) ≈ ~400 pages/s avg (plan ~800 peak) Page size (2025) P50 ≈ 138 KB, mean ≈ 242 KB the classic \"100 KB\" is dated — pages grew ~3–5× Storage 1B × ~250 KB, 5-yr retention ≈ 250 TB/crawl → tens of PB retai","keywords":["capacity","estimation"]},{"url":"core/web-crawler.html#loop","page":"Design a web crawler","section":"The crawl loop (BFS over the link graph)","tool":"core","text":"03 The crawl loop (BFS over the link graph) The pipeline (Mercator-style): the frontier hands out a URL (politely), a fetcher downloads it, a parser extracts links, dedup decides what's new, and unseen URLs flow back to the frontier — a breadth-first traversal of the web's link graph. Frontier → fet","keywords":["the","crawl","loop","bfs","over","link","graph"]},{"url":"core/web-crawler.html#frontier","page":"Design a web crawler","section":"The URL frontier (Mercator)","tool":"core","text":"04 The URL frontier (Mercator) Two FIFO stages do priority and politeness at once: Front queues — priority One queue per priority band; a URL's priority comes from its importance / change rate. Selection is biased toward higher-priority queues. This is \"BFS with priorities.\" Back queues — politeness","keywords":["the","url","frontier","mercator"]},{"url":"core/web-crawler.html#dedup","page":"Design a web crawler","section":"Dedup: URL-seen & near-duplicate content","tool":"core","text":"05 Dedup: URL-seen & near-duplicate content URL-seen across tens of billions of URLs would need hundreds of GB in a hash set — a Bloom filter answers it in a fraction of the space, accepting a tiny false-positive rate (≈10 bits/URL → ~1%; ~14–15 bits → ~0.1–0.2%) where a rare FP just skips a page (n","keywords":["dedup","url","seen","near","duplicate","content","import","math","mmh3","from","bitarray","class","BloomFilter","def","__init__","self","fp","max","int","log","bits","hashes","setall","_idx","item","for","in","range","yield","hash","per","seed","add","contains","False","definitely","absent","return","all","True"]},{"url":"core/web-crawler.html#politeness","page":"Design a web crawler","section":"Politeness, traps & freshness","tool":"core","text":"06 Politeness, traps & freshness robots.txt (RFC 9309) Standardizes only User-agent / Allow / Disallow (parse ≥500 KiB, cache ≤24h, treat 5xx as full-disallow). Crawl-delay and Sitemap are NOT in the standard — a common interview gotcha. Per-host rate-limit anyway (Mercator's adaptive \"next = now + ","keywords":["politeness","traps","freshness"]},{"url":"core/web-crawler.html#failure","page":"Design a web crawler","section":"Failure modes & distributed coordination","tool":"core","text":"07 Failure modes & distributed coordination 🧵 Fetcher crash Fetchers are stateless — restart and re-pull from the frontier. In-flight URLs need at-least-once redelivery (visibility timeout / un-acked offset). 💾 Frontier durability (the critical one) Lose the frontier and you lose the crawl's progr","keywords":["failure","modes","distributed","coordination"]},{"url":"core/web-crawler.html#ops","page":"Design a web crawler","section":"2026 wrinkles, observability & cost","tool":"core","text":"08 2026 wrinkles, observability & cost JS rendering Many pages need a headless-Chromium render (Google's two-phase fetch-then-render) costing ~1–2 orders of magnitude more CPU than a raw fetch — render selectively , gated by crawl budget. (Note: most AI crawlers — GPTBot, ClaudeBot — don't render JS","keywords":["wrinkles","observability","cost"]},{"url":"core/web-crawler.html#recap","page":"Design a web crawler","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"core","text":"09 Trade-offs & interview recap Why is a Bloom filter a good fit for the crawler's \"have I seen this URL?\" check? It never makes mistakes It uses tiny memory for billions of URLs; a rare false positive just skips a page, which is acceptable It stores the full page content Right: Bloom filters trade ","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"core/notification-system.html","page":"Design a notification system","section":"Design a notification system.","tool":"core","text":"Design a notification system. A shared pipe that takes \"notify user X about Y\" and reliably delivers it across push, SMS, email, and in-app — fast, deduplicated, and without each team rebuilding queues and retries. It's a masterclass in asynchronous, decoupled design, and the two ideas that separate","keywords":["design","notification","system"]},{"url":"core/notification-system.html#requirements","page":"Design a notification system","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"core","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Accept a request from any service; resolve template + user preferences/opt-outs; deliver via the right channel(s); expose delivery status (provider webhooks). 📐 Non-functional High throughput with spiky load (a blast = millions at once), at-least-once delivery w","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"core/notification-system.html#estimate","page":"Design a notification system","section":"Capacity estimation","tool":"core","text":"02 Capacity estimation Model a 10M-user blast you want drained in an hour, fanning out push + email: Quantity Assumption Result Ingest rate 10M ÷ 3,600 s, peak 3–5× ~2,800/s avg → size ingest for ~10–14k/s Channel fan-out push + email per user 10M recipients → ~20M channel sends Push ceiling (FCM) 6","keywords":["capacity","estimation"]},{"url":"core/notification-system.html#api","page":"Design a notification system","section":"API","tool":"core","text":"03 API Accept fast ( 202 ), do all real work async behind queues: POST /v1/notifications Idempotency-Key: … · { user_id, template_id, data, channels?, priority } → 202 { id, status:queued } GET /v1/notifications/{id} → per-channel delivery status (updated by provider webhooks) PUT /v1/users/{id}/pre","keywords":["api"]},{"url":"core/notification-system.html#model","page":"Design a notification system","section":"Data model","tool":"core","text":"04 Data model notification id, user_id, template_id, priority, payload, status, dedup_key. outbox id, event, payload, published_at — written in the same transaction as the notification (see §6). delivery_attempt append-only audit log: notification_id, channel, provider, provider_msg_id, status, erro","keywords":["data","model"]},{"url":"core/notification-system.html#architecture","page":"Design a notification system","section":"Queues all the way down","tool":"core","text":"05 Queues all the way down A thin API persists + enqueues; a router resolves preferences/templates and fans out to per-channel queues (with priority sub-lanes); channel workers call the providers. Per-channel isolation matters because providers have wildly different ceilings — a shared queue lets th","keywords":["queues","all","the","way","down"]},{"url":"core/notification-system.html#reliability","page":"Design a notification system","section":"Reliability: idempotency, retries & the outbox","tool":"core","text":"06 Reliability: idempotency, retries & the outbox Delivery is at-least-once, never exactly-once (impossible across a network — the two-generals problem). So make the consumer idempotent: claim a dedup key before calling the provider , with a TTL bounding the window. idempotent_send.sql Copy -- Atomi","keywords":["reliability","idempotency","retries","the","outbox","Atomically","claim","dedup","key","only","first","caller","proceeds","INSERT","INTO","dedup_keys","dedup_key","notification_id","created_at","VALUES","now","ON","CONFLICT","DO","NOTHING","RETURNING","row","time","call","provider","then","record","provider_msg_id","rows","duplicate","skip","already","sent","in","flight"]},{"url":"core/notification-system.html#channels","page":"Design a notification system","section":"Per-channel concerns & prioritization","tool":"core","text":"07 Per-channel concerns & prioritization 📲 Push (APNs/FCM) Tokens rotate — prune on invalid-token feedback. APNs payload ≤4 KB over persistent HTTP/2. FCM per-device throttle: 240/min, 5,000/hr. ✉️ Email deliverability Authenticate with SPF + DKIM (+ DMARC). Bounce/complaint handling is reputation-","keywords":["per","channel","concerns","prioritization"]},{"url":"core/notification-system.html#ops","page":"Design a notification system","section":"Failure, multi-region, observability & cost","tool":"core","text":"08 Failure, multi-region, observability & cost Failure modes Provider outage → a secondary provider per channel + circuit breaker (push is harder — APNs/FCM are sole upstreams, so degrade gracefully). Backlog → monitor consumer lag, shed/queue marketing to protect transactional. Multi-region Route v","keywords":["failure","multi","region","observability","cost"]},{"url":"core/notification-system.html#recap","page":"Design a notification system","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"core","text":"09 Trade-offs & interview recap Why give each channel (push/SMS/email) its own queue and worker pool rather than one shared pipeline? It looks more complex, which impresses interviewers So one slow or rate-limited provider only backs up its own channel instead of head-of-line-blocking the others Bec","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"core/news-feed.html","page":"Design a news feed (Twitter)","section":"Design a news feed.","tool":"core","text":"Design a news feed. A stream of recent posts from everyone you follow. It's the most iconic system-design question because it forces the field's most famous trade-off — fan-out on write vs read : do the work when someone posts, or when someone reads? Get that right, handle the celebrity who breaks i","keywords":["design","news","feed"]},{"url":"core/news-feed.html#requirements","page":"Design a news feed (Twitter)","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"core","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Post. View a home timeline (posts from people you follow). Follow/unfollow. Start chronological; ranking is a layer on top. 📐 Non-functional Extremely read-heavy (~100:1) — people scroll far more than they post. Timelines load < 200 ms. Eventual consistency is f","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"core/news-feed.html#api","page":"Design a news feed (Twitter)","section":"API & data model","tool":"core","text":"02 API & data model POST /v1/posts { text, media_ids?, reply_to?, idempotency_key } → 201 { post_id, created_at } GET /v1/timeline/home?limit=20&cursor=… → { items[], next_cursor } · cursor, never offset PUT /v1/follows/{target} follow / unfollow → 204 posts sharded by author_id (co-locates an autho","keywords":["api","data","model"]},{"url":"core/news-feed.html#tradeoff","page":"Design a news feed (Twitter)","section":"The core decision: fan-out","tool":"core","text":"03 The core decision: fan-out Push the work to write time (precompute every follower's timeline) or read time (assemble on demand)? Drag through the trade-off:","keywords":["the","core","decision","fan","out"]},{"url":"core/news-feed.html#architecture","page":"Design a news feed (Twitter)","section":"Architecture — fan-out on write","tool":"core","text":"04 Architecture — fan-out on write For most users, precompute. On post, the write service persists to the source of truth, then an async fan-out worker pushes the post id into each follower's timeline list — so the author's request returns instantly and each follower's read is a single lookup. Post ","keywords":["architecture","fan","out","on","write"]},{"url":"core/news-feed.html#celebrity","page":"Design a news feed (Twitter)","section":"The celebrity problem → hybrid","tool":"core","text":"05 The celebrity problem → hybrid Fan-out on write breaks for celebrities A 100M-follower account would trigger 100M Redis writes per post — at ~1 ms each that's ~28 hours to deliver one post. The fix is a hybrid gated by a follower-count threshold (a runtime config flag, not an architecture change)","keywords":["the","celebrity","problem","hybrid","CELEB_THRESHOLD","CAP","_000","800","def","on_post_created","ev","fan","out","worker","Kafka","consumer","if","graph","follower_count","author","redis","lpush","recent","post_id","celeb","park","don","ltrim","return","for","batch","in","iter_followers","regular","push","to","each","follower","pipe","pipeline"]},{"url":"core/news-feed.html#ranking","page":"Design a news feed (Twitter)","section":"Ranking (the 2026 reality: ML, not chronological)","tool":"core","text":"06 Ranking (the 2026 reality: ML, not chronological) The default \"For You\" feed on X, Instagram, and Threads is ML-ranked (chronological survives as an opt-in tab). Fan-out produces candidates ; ranking decides order , on the read path, as a multi-stage funnel: Candidate sourcing Narrow hundreds of ","keywords":["ranking","the","reality","ml","not","chronological"]},{"url":"core/news-feed.html#failure","page":"Design a news feed (Twitter)","section":"Failure modes, multi-region & cost","tool":"core","text":"07 Failure modes, multi-region & cost 🌊 Fan-out backlog A celebrity past the threshold or a spike backs up Kafka → \"post-to-visible\" balloons. Mitigate with the threshold itself, per-author rate limits, priority lanes, autoscaled workers, and a DLQ. Alert on consumer lag. 🧊 Timeline cache loss Red","keywords":["failure","modes","multi","region","cost"]},{"url":"core/news-feed.html#recap","page":"Design a news feed (Twitter)","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"core","text":"08 Trade-offs & interview recap A feed is 100:1 read-heavy and most users have modest follower counts. What's the right default, and the exception? Fan-out on read for everyone — writes stay cheap Fan-out on write by default (cheap reads), but pull at read time for celebrities to avoid fan-out storm","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"core/file-sync.html","page":"Design Dropbox / file sync","section":"Design file sync.","tool":"core","text":"Design file sync. Edit a file on your laptop and seconds later it's on your phone. Behind that simplicity is the defining split: content (big, immutable, content-addressed blobs in object storage) is handled completely differently from metadata (small, structured, strongly-consistent rows in a DB). ","keywords":["design","file","sync"]},{"url":"core/file-sync.html#requirements","page":"Design Dropbox / file sync","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"core","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Upload/download (chunked, resumable); sync across a user's devices; share; version history; work offline and reconcile on reconnect. 📐 Non-functional Exabyte-scale storage; minimise bandwidth (never re-upload a whole file for a one-line change); 12-nines durabil","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"core/file-sync.html#estimate","page":"Design Dropbox / file sync","section":"Capacity estimation","tool":"core","text":"02 Capacity estimation Real anchors (Dropbox): 700M+ users, multiple EB stored, >600k drives, >12-nines durability. Model 500M registered / 100M MAU: Quantity Assumption Result Logical storage 500M × 50 GB/user ≈ 25 EB logical After dedup + EC ~2× dedup, erasure-coded ~1.4× ≈ ~13 EB physical (every ","keywords":["capacity","estimation"]},{"url":"core/file-sync.html#api","page":"Design Dropbox / file sync","section":"API (chunked, resumable, dedup-first)","tool":"core","text":"03 API (chunked, resumable, dedup-first) POST /need_blocks { manifest:[hash…] } → { missing:[hash…] } · dedup BEFORE bytes move POST /upload_session/{start,append,finish} resumable: re-send from last acked offset after a crash GET /list_folder/continue?cursor=… · /longpoll?cursor=… paginated delta +","keywords":["api","chunked","resumable","dedup","first"]},{"url":"core/file-sync.html#model","page":"Design Dropbox / file sync","section":"Data model","tool":"core","text":"04 Data model file / version file = (namespace, parent, name); each version is immutable and points at a manifest . Versioning is free. manifest the ordered list of chunk hashes — a Merkle \"recipe\" for a file version. Diffing two manifests instantly identifies changed chunks. chunk hash PK (content-","keywords":["data","model"]},{"url":"core/file-sync.html#chunking","page":"Design Dropbox / file sync","section":"Chunking: why content-defined beats fixed-size","tool":"core","text":"05 Chunking: why content-defined beats fixed-size Fixed-size chunking (cut every 4 MB by offset) breaks on insertion : add one byte at the front and every later boundary shifts, so every chunk's hash changes — delta sync sees the whole file as new. Content-defined chunking (CDC) places boundaries by","keywords":["chunking","why","content","defined","beats","fixed","size","MIN","AVG","MAX","reference","bits","log2","MASK_S","strict","below","target","smaller","chunks","MASK_L","loose","above","larger","GEAR","rand64","for","in","range","256","table","shared","by","all","peers","def","next_cut","data","start","end","min"]},{"url":"core/file-sync.html#architecture","page":"Design Dropbox / file sync","section":"Metadata/content split & the sync flow","tool":"core","text":"06 Metadata/content split & the sync flow Two services: the metadata service (DB — needs transactions, strong consistency, low-latency point reads) tracks files/versions/manifests; the block service stores chunks in cheap, immutable, throughput-oriented object storage . Different durability/latency/","keywords":["metadata","content","split","the","sync","flow"]},{"url":"core/file-sync.html#failure","page":"Design Dropbox / file sync","section":"Conflicts, failure & the ordering invariant","tool":"core","text":"07 Conflicts, failure & the ordering invariant 🔀 Conflicts Two offline edits → two versions. File sync keeps both (\"conflicted copy\"), never silently merging opaque bytes. OT/CRDT merge is only for collaborative documents (Docs/Paper), not binary blobs. ⛓️ Block-before-metadata Always durably write","keywords":["conflicts","failure","the","ordering","invariant"]},{"url":"core/file-sync.html#ops","page":"Design Dropbox / file sync","section":"Multi-region, observability & cost","tool":"core","text":"08 Multi-region, observability & cost Multi-region Erasure-code blobs across zones (Dropbox replicates a fresh block to a second zone within ~1 s, then EC); accept uploads at the nearest PoP; pin a namespace's metadata to a home region for low-latency commits + residency. Observability & SLOs Sync l","keywords":["multi","region","observability","cost"]},{"url":"core/file-sync.html#recap","page":"Design Dropbox / file sync","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"core","text":"09 Trade-offs & interview recap You change one line in a 2 GB video project file. With content-defined chunking + delta sync, what gets uploaded? The entire 2 GB file again Only the chunk(s) whose contents (and therefore hash) changed — a few MB Nothing — edits aren't synced Right: only the chunks w","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"core/chat.html","page":"Design a chat system (WhatsApp)","section":"Design a chat system.","tool":"core","text":"Design a chat system. Real-time messaging flips request/response: the server must push a message the instant it arrives. That one requirement pulls in persistent connections (WebSockets), a registry to find which gateway holds a user's socket, careful delivery/ordering guarantees, and presence. The ","keywords":["design","chat","system"]},{"url":"core/chat.html#requirements","page":"Design a chat system (WhatsApp)","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"core","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional 1:1 and group messaging; delivered/read receipts; online presence; history; offline delivery (deliver on reconnect); multi-device. 📐 Non-functional Feels instant; hundreds of millions of concurrent connections; messages never lost and ordered within a conversati","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"core/chat.html#estimate","page":"Design a chat system (WhatsApp)","section":"Capacity estimation","tool":"core","text":"02 Capacity estimation Quantity Assumption Result Message rate 100B msg/day (WhatsApp scale), peak 3–5× ~1.16M/s avg → ~3.5–6M msg/s peak Concurrent connections ~300M online at 1M conns/box → ~300+ gateway boxes (+ redundancy) Memory/connection epoll + zero-copy ≈ 4–5 KB (naive 10 MB!) 1M conns ≈ ~4","keywords":["capacity","estimation"]},{"url":"core/chat.html#api","page":"Design a chat system (WhatsApp)","section":"API & transport","tool":"core","text":"03 API & transport WS send(conv_id, client_msg_id, ciphertext) client → server over the open socket WS receive / delivery_ack / read_receipt server → client push; receipts fan back to sender WebSocket is the fit: one full-duplex TLS connection, low per-message overhead, server-initiated push. Long-p","keywords":["api","transport"]},{"url":"core/chat.html#model","page":"Design a chat system (WhatsApp)","section":"Data model","tool":"core","text":"04 Data model messages (wide-column) partition key (conversation_id, time_bucket) , clustering key = sequence/timestamp. \"Last N in conversation X\" is a single-partition range scan, not a scatter-gather. Why wide-column (Cassandra/ScyllaDB/HBase): append-heavy, time-ordered, range-read — exactly the","keywords":["data","model"]},{"url":"core/chat.html#routing","page":"Design a chat system (WhatsApp)","section":"Routing a message across gateways","tool":"core","text":"05 Routing a message across gateways The core question: the recipient's socket lives on gateway G2, the sender on G1. On connect, each gateway writes user → {gateway, conn} into a session registry (Redis / consistent-hashed store). To deliver, look up the recipient's gateway and route via a pub/sub ","keywords":["routing","message","across","gateways","def","handle_send","sender","conv_id","client_msg_id","ciphertext","if","dedup","seen","at","least","once","effectively","return","ack","server_id","seq","seq_allocator","next","monotonic","PER","CONVERSATION","cheap","msg","Message","ts","now","store","append","STORE","BEFORE","ACK","durable","first","record","id"]},{"url":"core/chat.html#delivery","page":"Design a chat system (WhatsApp)","section":"Delivery, ordering & groups","tool":"core","text":"06 Delivery, ordering & groups At-least-once + dedup Reconnects cause resends; the client_msg_id is the idempotency key — \"already seen → skip + re-ack.\" Ordering A per-conversation monotonic sequence (not global — users only order within a conversation, so it's one cheap counter per partition). Cli","keywords":["delivery","ordering","groups"]},{"url":"core/chat.html#presence","page":"Design a chat system (WhatsApp)","section":"Presence & failure modes","tool":"core","text":"07 Presence & failure modes 🟢 Presence is expensive Heartbeats update last_seen in Redis. One presence change notifies all F subscribers → N×F amplification that dwarfs message traffic (presence fires far more often than messages). Mitigate: reuse the existing socket for presence frames, coalesce/b","keywords":["presence","failure","modes"]},{"url":"core/chat.html#ops","page":"Design a chat system (WhatsApp)","section":"Multi-region, E2E, observability & cost","tool":"core","text":"08 Multi-region, E2E, observability & cost Multi-region Connect to the nearest gateway region (GeoDNS/anycast); the session registry is region-aware so cross-region pairs route over the backplane. Messages favour availability + eventual consistency (per-conversation order preserved via seq); Cassand","keywords":["multi","region","e2e","observability","cost"]},{"url":"core/chat.html#recap","page":"Design a chat system (WhatsApp)","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"core","text":"09 Trade-offs & interview recap Why are WebSockets preferred over plain HTTP request/response for chat delivery? They use less bandwidth per message header They keep a persistent, bidirectional connection so the server can push a message the instant it arrives, without the client polling HTTP can't ","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"advanced/uber.html","page":"Design Uber / ride-hailing","section":"Design ride-hailing.","tool":"advanced","text":"Design ride-hailing. Match a rider to a nearby driver in seconds while millions of drivers stream GPS continuously. The signature challenge is geospatial — \"find the closest available drivers to this point\" — which ordinary databases are bad at. The deeper asymmetry: the write side (location updates","keywords":["design","ride","hailing"]},{"url":"advanced/uber.html#requirements","page":"Design Uber / ride-hailing","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"advanced","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Drivers publish location every ~4 s. A rider requests a trip; the system finds nearby drivers, offers the ride, tracks it. Plus ETA + surge pricing. 📐 Non-functional Enormous write volume; sub-second matching; real-time location to the rider; mostly regional — t","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"advanced/uber.html#estimate","page":"Design Uber / ride-hailing","section":"Capacity estimation","tool":"advanced","text":"02 Capacity estimation Quantity Assumption Result Location writes 5M online drivers ÷ 4 s ~1.25M updates/s — the write storm Live location state 5M × ~64 B ≈ ~320 MB RAM — tiny; the cost is write throughput, not size Match requests ~25M rides/day, peak 5–10× ≈ ~1.5–3k/s — 5+ orders below the write r","keywords":["capacity","estimation"]},{"url":"advanced/uber.html#api","page":"Design Uber / ride-hailing","section":"API & data model","tool":"advanced","text":"03 API & data model PUT updateLocation(driverId, lat, lng, ts) high-frequency, best-effort (204), idempotent-by-overwrite POST requestRide(riderId, pickup, productType) → { requestId, etaEstimate, fareEstimate } POST acceptOffer(driverId, requestId) → { tripId } | 409 ALREADY_TAKEN (must be lineariz","keywords":["api","data","model"]},{"url":"advanced/uber.html#geo","page":"Design Uber / ride-hailing","section":"The hard part: \"who's nearby?\"","tool":"advanced","text":"04 The hard part: \"who's nearby?\" Computing distance to every driver is O(N) per request — hopeless at thousands of requests/s. A spatial index turns it into O(1) cell lookup + O(k) neighbor scan : examine only the rider's cell and a ring of adjacent cells. Search the rider's cell + neighbours ⏮ ‹ ▶","keywords":["the","hard","part","who","nearby","import","h3","def","update_location","driver_id","lat","lng","res","write","path","move","cell","latlng_to_cell","old","driver_cell","get","if","cell_index","discard","add","H3","driverIds","nearby_drivers","want","max_k","read","ring","origin","found","for","in","range","grid_disk","cells","extend"]},{"url":"advanced/uber.html#architecture","page":"Design Uber / ride-hailing","section":"Architecture, matching & dispatch","tool":"advanced","text":"05 Architecture, matching & dispatch Two flows: a torrent of location updates into the in-memory geo-index (sharded across nodes by cell), and matching — read the index, rank candidates by ETA, offer the ride. Avoiding double-dispatch (one driver offered to two riders) needs a linearizable accept. R","keywords":["architecture","matching","dispatch"]},{"url":"advanced/uber.html#eta","page":"Design Uber / ride-hailing","section":"ETA, routing & surge","tool":"advanced","text":"06 ETA, routing & surge ETA & ranking Rank by road-network ETA, not Euclidean distance (\"0.5 km across a river\" can be a 10-min drive). ETA is the inner loop of every dispatch (tight p99 budget) — contraction hierarchies precompute shortcuts so a route is a few ms, and an ML model (Uber's DeepETA) p","keywords":["eta","routing","surge"]},{"url":"advanced/uber.html#failure","page":"Design Uber / ride-hailing","section":"Failure modes & geo-sharding","tool":"advanced","text":"07 Failure modes & geo-sharding 📍 Lost location update Stale position for ≤4 s — self-healing: the next tick overwrites it; treat fixes older than N intervals as offline. 🤝 Matching race Two riders, one driver → double-dispatch. Single-owner linearizable accept (consistent-hash the driver key to o","keywords":["failure","modes","geo","sharding"]},{"url":"advanced/uber.html#ops","page":"Design Uber / ride-hailing","section":"Observability & cost","tool":"advanced","text":"08 Observability & cost Metrics & SLOs Match latency p99 (SLO < ~1 s), match success/fulfillment rate per city, location-update freshness (alert if % of drivers with a stale fix rises), ETA error (MAE vs actual → retrain trigger), surge accuracy, cluster membership churn. Cost Dominated by the durab","keywords":["observability","cost"]},{"url":"advanced/uber.html#recap","page":"Design Uber / ride-hailing","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"advanced","text":"09 Trade-offs & interview recap Why use a geohash/QuadTree/H3 index instead of computing the distance to every driver? It stores driver names more compactly It limits the search to a few nearby cells instead of scanning every driver — turning an O(N) scan into a small bounded lookup It makes GPS mor","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"advanced/youtube.html","page":"Design YouTube / video streaming","section":"Design video streaming.","tool":"advanced","text":"Design video streaming. Let anyone upload a video and let everyone, on any device and any connection, watch it smoothly. The bytes are gigantic, so two things dominate: a transcoding pipeline (one upload → many qualities/codecs, in parallel) and a CDN (serve from the edge). Egress bandwidth — not st","keywords":["design","video","streaming"]},{"url":"advanced/youtube.html#requirements","page":"Design YouTube / video streaming","section":"Requirements & capacity","tool":"advanced","text":"01 Requirements & capacity ✅ Functional Upload (resumable); process into streamable formats; watch with adaptive quality; search + recommend. 📐 Non-functional EB-scale storage, enormous read bandwidth, global low-latency playback, and cost control (egress dominates by 1–2 orders of magnitude). Anch","keywords":["requirements","capacity"]},{"url":"advanced/youtube.html#api","page":"Design YouTube / video streaming","section":"API & data model","tool":"advanced","text":"02 API & data model POST /upload?uploadType=resumable → Location: session URI · PUT chunks with Content-Range · 308 Resume Incomplete GET /manifest/{id}.m3u8 | .mpd the bitrate ladder (HLS/DASH) GET /seg/{id}/{rendition}/{n}.m4s immutable CMAF segment — long CDN TTL Polyglot persistence: video metad","keywords":["api","data","model"]},{"url":"advanced/youtube.html#transcode","page":"Design YouTube / video streaming","section":"The upload & transcoding pipeline","tool":"advanced","text":"03 The upload & transcoding pipeline The raw upload lands in object storage; the creator sees \"processing…\" instantly. A DAG of jobs then chunks the video (GOP-aligned), transcodes each chunk across a farm in parallel, stitches, packages (CMAF), and warms the CDN — with thumbnails, captions, and QC ","keywords":["the","upload","transcoding","pipeline"]},{"url":"advanced/youtube.html#abr","page":"Design YouTube / video streaming","section":"Adaptive bitrate, codecs & the player","tool":"advanced","text":"04 Adaptive bitrate, codecs & the player The watch path is almost entirely CDN : the player fetches a small manifest (the bitrate ladder), then streams immutable segments from the nearest edge, switching quality per segment. Protocols: HLS (.m3u8) and MPEG-DASH (.mpd), converging on CMAF (one set of","keywords":["adaptive","bitrate","codecs","the","player","ladder","ascending","bitrate_bps","height","measured_bps","EWMA","of","recent","segment","throughput","def","pick_rendition","buffer_s","safety","low_buf","panic_buf","usable","headroom","for","variance","affordable","in","if","pick","else","lowest","rung","return","near","stall","drop","to","floor","cautious","bounded"]},{"url":"advanced/youtube.html#cdn","page":"Design YouTube / video streaming","section":"CDN, the edge & cost","tool":"advanced","text":"05 CDN, the edge & cost Multi-tier edge PoP → regional mid-tier (origin shield, collapses duplicate misses) → origin/object storage. Immutable segments → long TTLs → very high hit rates. Head vs long tail Cache the popular head hot at the edge; the cold long tail lives in cheaper storage and is fetc","keywords":["cdn","the","edge","cost"]},{"url":"advanced/youtube.html#ops","page":"Design YouTube / video streaming","section":"Failure, multi-region & observability","tool":"advanced","text":"06 Failure, multi-region & observability 🔁 Transcode failure Jobs are idempotent (content-addressed outputs) + retried with backoff; chunk-level granularity re-runs only the failed chunk. Poison jobs → DLQ so one bad upload can't block the farm. 📤 Resumable upload Chunked + 308/Range resume surviv","keywords":["failure","multi","region","observability"]},{"url":"advanced/youtube.html#recap","page":"Design YouTube / video streaming","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"advanced","text":"07 Trade-offs & interview recap Why transcode each upload into many bitrates and chop it into short segments up front? To save the creator's upload bandwidth So the player can adapt quality to the viewer's connection mid-stream, and each segment is a cacheable CDN object Because video can't be store","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"advanced/payments.html","page":"Design a payment system","section":"Design a payment system.","tool":"advanced","text":"Design a payment system. Payments flip the usual priorities: correctness beats availability and speed — charging twice or losing a transaction is unacceptable. Two invariants drive everything: exactly-once charging (idempotency, so retries don't double-charge) and never creating or destroying money ","keywords":["design","payment","system"]},{"url":"advanced/payments.html#requirements","page":"Design a payment system","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"advanced","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Charge a customer, pay out merchants, refunds, talk to external networks/banks, expose a clear transaction history. 📐 Non-functional Strong consistency & correctness above all; full auditability; exactly-once effects despite retries and flaky external rails; dur","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"advanced/payments.html#api","page":"Design a payment system","section":"API & data model","tool":"advanced","text":"02 API & data model POST /v1/payments Idempotency-Key: 8f3c… · { amount (minor units int), currency, source, capture_method } POST /v1/payments/{id}/{capture,refund,cancel} cancel only pre-capture; refund only post-capture Payment intent id, idempotency_key, amounts as int64 minor units (never float","keywords":["api","data","model"]},{"url":"advanced/payments.html#idempotency","page":"Design a payment system","section":"Idempotency: the no-double-charge rule","tool":"advanced","text":"03 Idempotency: the no-double-charge rule A charge is a non-idempotent, irreversible external side effect. If a client retries after a lost response, a naive system fires a second charge. The fix: a client-supplied idempotency key ; the server stores the first request's result (success or failure) a","keywords":["idempotency","the","no","double","charge","rule","BEGIN","Claim","key","ON","CONFLICT","short","circuits","retry","INSERT","INTO","idempotency_keys","idempotency_key","user_id","recovery_point","locked_at","VALUES","started","now","DO","NOTHING","RETURNING","id","rows","exists","load","replay","stored","response","or","409","if","still","locked","One"]},{"url":"advanced/payments.html#ledger","page":"Design a payment system","section":"The double-entry ledger","tool":"advanced","text":"04 The double-entry ledger Every movement is a balanced pair — a debit and an equal credit — so the books always sum to zero and money is never created or lost. The journal is append-only and immutable : you never edit an entry, you add a reversing one. That's event sourcing for money: balance = a f","keywords":["the","double","entry","ledger"]},{"url":"advanced/payments.html#consistency","page":"Design a payment system","section":"Multi-step money movement","tool":"advanced","text":"05 Multi-step money movement Moving money across services (charge → ledger → payout) needs more than one local transaction. The options: And the workhorse for reliable events: the outbox pattern — in one local transaction write the ledger change and an outbox row; a relay (poller or Debezium CDC) pu","keywords":["multi","step","money","movement"]},{"url":"advanced/payments.html#reconcile","page":"Design a payment system","section":"Reconciliation & the unknown-state problem","tool":"advanced","text":"06 Reconciliation & the unknown-state problem Two independent systems (you and the bank) drift, so reconciliation compares your ledger against external truth. The 2026 best practice is three-way : internal ledger ↔ PSP settlement file ↔ bank statement — two-way recon gives false confidence (it never","keywords":["reconciliation","the","unknown","state","problem"]},{"url":"advanced/payments.html#ops","page":"Design a payment system","section":"Compliance, multi-region & observability","tool":"advanced","text":"07 Compliance, multi-region & observability PCI-DSS scope minimization Keep raw PAN out of your systems via tokenization + a dedicated vault ; network tokens (EMVCo / Apple-Google Pay DPANs) are the 2026 default and also lift auth rates (~2–5%) and cut fraud. 3DS2 / SCA (PSD2) Strong Customer Authen","keywords":["compliance","multi","region","observability"]},{"url":"advanced/payments.html#recap","page":"Design a payment system","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"advanced","text":"08 Trade-offs & interview recap A client's \"charge $50\" request times out and the client retries. How does the system avoid charging twice? It hopes the timeout meant the first charge failed The request carries an idempotency key; the server returns the stored result for a repeat key instead of char","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"advanced/llm-serving.html","page":"Design an LLM serving system","section":"Design an LLM serving system.","tool":"advanced","text":"Design an LLM serving system. Serving a large language model breaks the usual rules: work happens on scarce, expensive GPUs ; each request generates token by token over seconds; and throughput hinges on batching many requests together. The mental model to anchor on: generation has two phases — prefi","keywords":["design","an","llm","serving","system"]},{"url":"advanced/llm-serving.html#requirements","page":"Design an LLM serving system","section":"Requirements & what's different","tool":"advanced","text":"01 Requirements & what's different ✅ Functional Accept a prompt, run inference, stream tokens back as produced. Many concurrent users, variable-length prompts/outputs. 📐 Non-functional Low TTFT (time-to-first-token), high tokens/s, efficient use of very expensive GPUs (utilisation = cost), graceful","keywords":["requirements","what","different"]},{"url":"advanced/llm-serving.html#estimate","page":"Design an LLM serving system","section":"Capacity: the KV-cache budget","tool":"advanced","text":"02 Capacity: the KV-cache budget GPU memory = model weights + KV cache + activations. The KV cache is the variable that decides how many concurrent tokens you can serve: kv_cache.txt Copy KV_bytes = 2 (K and V) × layers × kv_heads × head_dim × bytes × seq_len × batch Llama-3-70B (80 layers, 8 KV hea","keywords":["capacity","the","kv","cache","budget","KV_bytes","and","layers","kv_heads","head_dim","bytes","seq_len","batch","Llama","KV","heads","via","GQA","128","BF16","GB","context","MB","per","token","one","sequence","of","alone"]},{"url":"advanced/llm-serving.html#api","page":"Design an LLM serving system","section":"API & the request path","tool":"advanced","text":"03 API & the request path OpenAI-compatible is the de-facto standard: messages + sampling params + stream: true , emitting data-only SSE deltas (server→client, plain HTTP, proxy-friendly) ending with [DONE] . The router does auth, rate-limiting, and — increasingly — prefix/KV-aware routing (send req","keywords":["api","the","request","path"]},{"url":"advanced/llm-serving.html#batching","page":"Design an LLM serving system","section":"Continuous batching","tool":"advanced","text":"04 Continuous batching Static batching fixes a batch and runs it to completion — so the whole batch waits for the longest sequence (head-of-line blocking) while finished sequences leave their GPU slots idle. Continuous (in-flight) batching operates per token-step: add waiting requests and evict fini","keywords":["continuous","batching","running","waiting","deque","def","step","SCHEDULE","top","up","the","batch","from","queue","under","token","budget","while","and","kv_cache","has_free_blocks","token_budget_left","seq","popleft","if","not","allocate","enough","KV","blocks","appendleft","break","append","prefill","happens","this","FORWARD","one","fused","pass"]},{"url":"advanced/llm-serving.html#kvcache","page":"Design an LLM serving system","section":"KV cache & PagedAttention","tool":"advanced","text":"05 KV cache & PagedAttention Classic systems store each sequence's KV in one contiguous buffer pre-sized to the max length — wasting 60–80% of KV memory to internal fragmentation. PagedAttention (vLLM) treats KV like OS virtual memory: fixed-size blocks (16 tokens), non-contiguous in HBM, mapped via","keywords":["kv","cache","pagedattention"]},{"url":"advanced/llm-serving.html#modern","page":"Design an LLM serving system","section":"2026 techniques, autoscaling & failure","tool":"advanced","text":"06 2026 techniques, autoscaling & failure 🔀 Disaggregated prefill/decode The big 2025→2026 shift: prefill is compute-bound, decode is bandwidth-bound, so co-locating them interferes. Put them on separate GPU pools (transfer KV over the interconnect) → up to ~7.4× more requests / 12.6× tighter SLO. ","keywords":["techniques","autoscaling","failure"]},{"url":"advanced/llm-serving.html#ops","page":"Design an LLM serving system","section":"Multi-region, cost & observability","tool":"advanced","text":"07 Multi-region, cost & observability Cost GPU cost dominates — optimise throughput per dollar (cost/M tokens) , not raw latency. Levers: quantization (FP8→FP4), continuous batching + high KV utilisation, prefix caching (skip repeated prefill), disaggregation, bigger batches for throughput tiers. Ti","keywords":["multi","region","cost","observability"]},{"url":"advanced/llm-serving.html#recap","page":"Design an LLM serving system","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"advanced","text":"08 Trade-offs & interview recap Why is continuous batching so central to serving LLMs efficiently? It reduces the model's accuracy to save time Decode is bandwidth-bound, so batching amortizes the weight load; adding/evicting sequences per step keeps the expensive GPU full despite variable output le","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"advanced/vector-recommender.html","page":"Design vector search & a recommender","section":"Design vector search & a recommender.","tool":"advanced","text":"Design vector search & a recommender. Modern search and recommendations rest on embeddings : turn every item and query into a point in high-dimensional space so \"similar in meaning\" becomes \"close together.\" Finding good results is then \"find the nearest points\" — a job for approximate nearest-neigh","keywords":["design","vector","search","recommender"]},{"url":"advanced/vector-recommender.html#embeddings","page":"Design vector search & a recommender","section":"Embeddings & nearest neighbours","tool":"advanced","text":"01 Embeddings & nearest neighbours Embeddings are dense semantic vectors; nearby vectors are semantically similar (cosine / dot product). \"Search\" and \"recommend\" become the same operation: find the nearest points to the query/user. Find the k nearest items to a query ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× Exact ","keywords":["embeddings","nearest","neighbours"]},{"url":"advanced/vector-recommender.html#estimate","page":"Design vector search & a recommender","section":"Capacity estimation","tool":"advanced","text":"02 Capacity estimation Quantity Assumption Result Raw vectors 1B × 768 × 4 B (float32) ≈ 3.07 TB raw HNSW resident + graph links (~1.5–2×) ≈ 3–5 TB RAM (the cost driver) Product quantization 768-dim → 96 B/vector (~32×) ≈ 96 GB (lossy → re-rank with full vectors) DiskANN (SSD) compressed in RAM, ful","keywords":["capacity","estimation"]},{"url":"advanced/vector-recommender.html#api","page":"Design vector search & a recommender","section":"API & data model","tool":"advanced","text":"03 API & data model GET search(query, k, filter) query → embed → filtered ANN top-K POST recommend(user_id, context, k) user tower → embed → ANN → rank → filter PUT upsert(item_id, embedding, metadata) add/replace vector + payload (deletes are tombstones) ANN index + vector/metadata store vectors + ","keywords":["api","data","model"]},{"url":"advanced/vector-recommender.html#ann","page":"Design vector search & a recommender","section":"ANN indexes: recall vs latency vs memory","tool":"advanced","text":"04 ANN indexes: recall vs latency vs memory The three families span the cost/quality triangle (recall ↑ costs latency ↑ and/or memory ↑; the curve steepens sharply from 0.95→0.99): Knobs: HNSW's M / ef_search , IVF's nlist / nprobe . Pushing the last few points of recall (0.95→0.99) can cost 3–5× la","keywords":["ann","indexes","recall","vs","latency","memory"]},{"url":"advanced/vector-recommender.html#pipeline","page":"Design vector search & a recommender","section":"The two-stage recommender","tool":"advanced","text":"05 The two-stage recommender You can't run an expensive cross-feature ranker over a billion items per request. So the funnel: candidate generation (cheap, recall-oriented ANN — billions → hundreds) then ranking (expensive deep model scores those hundreds with rich features → dozens). In practice mul","keywords":["the","two","stage","recommender","Item","embeddings","are","precomputed","OFFLINE","for","whole","catalog","and","loaded","into","ANN","Only","user","tower","runs","online","new","items","need","just","an","item","pass","upsert","no","model","retrain","so","freshness","is","cheap","def","recommend","user_tower","features"]},{"url":"advanced/vector-recommender.html#freshness","page":"Design vector search & a recommender","section":"Freshness, the stack & RAG","tool":"advanced","text":"06 Freshness, the stack & RAG Online vs offline Offline batch embed + index rebuild is simplest but stale (hours); HNSW supports incremental inserts , while IVF needs periodic re-clustering. Deletes are hard — removing an HNSW node breaks graph links, so use tombstones + periodic compaction (frequen","keywords":["freshness","the","stack","rag"]},{"url":"advanced/vector-recommender.html#ops","page":"Design vector search & a recommender","section":"Failure, multi-region, observability & cost","tool":"advanced","text":"07 Failure, multi-region, observability & cost 🧊 Stale embeddings Retrain the model but not re-embed the catalog → query and item vectors live in different spaces → silent recall collapse. Version embeddings; re-embed on model change. ❄️ Cold-start New user (no vector) / new item (not indexed). Mit","keywords":["failure","multi","region","observability","cost"]},{"url":"advanced/vector-recommender.html#recap","page":"Design vector search & a recommender","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"advanced","text":"08 Trade-offs & interview recap Why split a recommender into candidate generation then ranking, instead of ranking everything? Ranking models can't handle embeddings Scoring a precise model over billions of items per request is far too slow; cheap ANN narrows to a few hundred, which the expensive mo","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture.","tool":"expert","text":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture. One codebase and one fleet serving thousands of isolated customers. The defining decision is isolation vs density : how strongly do you separate tenants, and how many can you pack onto shared infrastructure? Get it wrong one way and you bleed money; the other","keywords":["design","multi","tenant","saas","architecture"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html#requirements","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"expert","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional One application serves many tenants (companies/workspaces). Each sees only its own data, config, and branding. Onboard a tenant in seconds; offboard (export + delete) cleanly. 📐 Non-functional Hard tenant isolation (no cross-tenant leak — ever), high density/low","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html#isolation","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"Isolation models: silo, pool, bridge","tool":"expert","text":"02 Isolation models: silo, pool, bridge AWS's SaaS Lens names three patterns — and the key insight is you can apply them per service , not just whole-app: Tiered isolation is the staff answer: the same code, and the tenant's tier in the catalog decides its footprint — pool for self-serve, silo for e","keywords":["isolation","models","silo","pool","bridge"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html#estimate","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"Capacity & density","tool":"expert","text":"03 Capacity & density Density is why pooling exists. Worked example for 10,000 small tenants (a few MB, low QPS each): Model Packing ~DB cost/tenant/mo Pool (shared tables) 10,000 tenants in 1 DB ~$180/mo ÷ 10k ≈ $0.018 Silo (DB per tenant) ~100 small DBs/instance → ~100 instances* ~$18,000/mo ÷ 10k","keywords":["capacity","density"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html#model","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"Data model: tenant_id everywhere","tool":"expert","text":"04 Data model: tenant_id everywhere The discriminator Every business table carries tenant_id NOT NULL ; it's the leading column of every composite index and PK ( (tenant_id, id) ) and the shard/distribution key. FKs stay within a tenant. The tenant catalog (control plane) Authoritative registry: ten","keywords":["data","model","tenant_id","everywhere"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html#routing","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"Tenant routing & context propagation","tool":"expert","text":"05 Tenant routing & context propagation Resolve the tenant once at the edge, then enforce it automatically at every layer. Never trust a tenant id from the request body — derive it from the subdomain, a verified JWT claim, or the API key. acme.app.com → resolve tenant → scoped query ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.","keywords":["tenant","routing","context","propagation"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html#rls","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"Pool isolation with Postgres RLS","tool":"expert","text":"06 Pool isolation with Postgres RLS In the pool model, the discriminator is just an app convention — one missing WHERE tenant_id anywhere = a breach. Push the predicate into the database with Row-Level Security so the engine enforces it even if the app forgets. rls.sql Copy -- App connects as a NON-","keywords":["pool","isolation","with","postgres","rls","App","connects","as","NON","OWNER","non","superuser","role","owners","superusers","bypass","RLS","ALTER","TABLE","events","ENABLE","ROW","LEVEL","SECURITY","FORCE","apply","even","to","the","table","owner","CREATE","POLICY","tenant_isolation","ON","TO","application","USING","tenant_id","NULLIF"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html#noisy","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"The noisy-neighbour problem","tool":"expert","text":"07 The noisy-neighbour problem Pooling shares CPU, memory, disk IO, connections, and locks — so one tenant's spike can starve the rest. Mitigations, by contention vector: Per-tenant rate limits / quotas Token-bucket at the gateway; return 429 early (backpressure) rather than degrading everyone. Sale","keywords":["the","noisy","neighbour","problem"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html#cells","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"Sharding tenants & cell architecture","tool":"expert","text":"08 Sharding tenants & cell architecture Shard by tenant_id (e.g. Citus distribution column) so all of a tenant's rows + co-distributed tables live on one node → local joins, no cross-node traffic; reference tables replicate everywhere. A tenant can be rebalanced by relocating its shard. Cells bound ","keywords":["sharding","tenants","cell","architecture"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html#migrations","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"Migrations & schema drift","tool":"expert","text":"09 Migrations & schema drift 🟢 Pool Migration runs once for everyone — the operational win. Risk: a long-locking DDL hits all tenants at once → use online expand-contract, CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY , batched backfills. 🟠 Schema/DB per tenant The same migration must run across thousands of schemas/","keywords":["migrations","schema","drift"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html#compliance","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"Compliance, residency & per-tenant keys","tool":"expert","text":"10 Compliance, residency & per-tenant keys Residency Silo makes it trivial (host the tenant DB in-region). In pool/cell, pin residency at the cell/shard level — group EU tenants into EU cells, US into US cells. Per-tenant encryption / BYOK One customer-managed KMS key per tenant (~$1–3/mo each → key","keywords":["compliance","residency","per","tenant","keys"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html#ops","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"Observability & cost","tool":"expert","text":"11 Observability & cost Cross-tenant leak detection RLS as a backstop; automated tests asserting tenant A can't read tenant B ; lint/ORM guards that every query carries a tenant predicate; alert if any query returns rows whose tenant_id ≠ the session tenant. Tenant-aware telemetry Stamp tenant_id on","keywords":["observability","cost"]},{"url":"expert/multi-tenancy.html#recap","page":"Design a multi-tenant SaaS architecture","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"expert","text":"12 Trade-offs & interview recap In the pool model, what's the single most dangerous failure, and the primary defense? A slow query; fix it with an index A query missing its tenant_id predicate leaks another tenant's data — defend with Postgres RLS (FORCED, non-owner role, SET LOCAL) plus app-layer s","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"expert/user-management.html","page":"Design a user management system","section":"Design a user management system.","tool":"expert","text":"Design a user management system. The service that owns the user as a durable entity — accounts, profiles, lifecycle — and that every other system foreign-keys to. The senior move is to draw the boundary first: this page owns who exists ; authentication owns how they prove it and authorization owns w","keywords":["design","user","management","system"]},{"url":"expert/user-management.html#requirements","page":"Design a user management system","section":"Requirements & the scope boundary","tool":"expert","text":"01 Requirements & the scope boundary ✅ Functional Create accounts, verify email, manage profile/preferences, deactivate, delete (GDPR), export data. Self-service and admin/DSAR flows. 📐 Non-functional The user object is a fan-in dependency (everything reads it) → must be highly available + cached; ","keywords":["requirements","the","scope","boundary"]},{"url":"expert/user-management.html#lifecycle","page":"Design a user management system","section":"The account lifecycle & email verification","tool":"expert","text":"02 The account lifecycle & email verification An account is a state machine: invited → registered → (verify) → active → suspended/locked → deactivated → deleted . Suspended (admin/policy) and locked (security, e.g. failed-login throttle) are distinct — different reactivation paths. Verification gate","keywords":["the","account","lifecycle","email","verification"]},{"url":"expert/user-management.html#model","page":"Design a user management system","section":"Data model: separate identity, profile & emails","tool":"expert","text":"03 Data model: separate identity, profile & emails Separate things that change at different rates and have different sensitivity: the small hot anchor , the security-critical identities , and the read-hot profile . users.sql Copy -- THE ANCHOR: small, hot, cached everywhere; FK target for the whole ","keywords":["data","model","separate","identity","profile","emails","THE","ANCHOR","small","hot","cached","everywhere","FK","target","for","the","whole","company","CREATE","TABLE","users","id","uuid","PRIMARY","KEY","DEFAULT","uuidv7","time","ordered","index","local","status","text","NOT","NULL","registered","active","suspended","created_at","timestamptz"]},{"url":"expert/user-management.html#api","page":"Design a user management system","section":"API","tool":"expert","text":"04 API POST /v1/users · /v1/emails/verify signup → 201 {status:registered}; verify token → active GET /v1/users/me · /v1/users/me/export read; export is async → signed download (Art. 20) DELETE /v1/users/me begin erasure: soft-delete → grace → anonymize Verification/recovery endpoints are rate-limit","keywords":["api"]},{"url":"expert/user-management.html#erasure","page":"Design a user management system","section":"GDPR/CCPA erasure: you can't just DELETE","tool":"expert","text":"05 GDPR/CCPA erasure: you can't just DELETE Financial ledgers, fraud/audit logs, and immutable event stores must survive (GDPR Art. 17(3) carves out legal-obligation + legal-claims retention). So \"right to erasure\" in practice = anonymize the PII in place and leave a tombstone , deleting only what y","keywords":["gdpr","ccpa","erasure","you","can","just","delete","BEGIN","UPDATE","users","SET","status","deleted","deleted_at","now","WHERE","id","uid","tombstone","Replace","PII","with","an","UNRECOVERABLE","placeholder","not","reversible","hash","emails","email","gen_random_uuid","anonymized","invalid","citext","verified_at","NULL","user_id","profiles","display_name","avatar_url"]},{"url":"expert/user-management.html#sessions","page":"Design a user management system","section":"Lifecycle → session revocation fan-out","tool":"expert","text":"06 Lifecycle → session revocation fan-out Token mechanics belong to the auth page ; what user-management owns is the trigger . On suspend/lock/deactivate/delete, emit a user.suspended event and revoke access. Because access tokens are short-lived JWTs you can't instantly kill one — so either revoke ","keywords":["lifecycle","session","revocation","fan","out"]},{"url":"expert/user-management.html#uniqueness","page":"Design a user management system","section":"Uniqueness, normalization & account merge","tool":"expert","text":"07 Uniqueness, normalization & account merge Email uniqueness Enforce with a DB UNIQUE constraint , not a read-then-write check — two concurrent signups both pass a SELECT and both INSERT ; only the constraint stops the dupe (handle 23505 gracefully). Normalization Treat email case-insensitively (st","keywords":["uniqueness","normalization","account","merge"]},{"url":"expert/user-management.html#scale","page":"Design a user management system","section":"Scale: the user service is fan-in","tool":"expert","text":"08 Scale: the user service is fan-in Quantity Assumption Result Population 100M users × ~250 B core ≈ 25 GB — RAM-resident; you don't shard for size Read QPS 20M DAU × ~50 user-resolving reads/day ≈ 11.6k/s avg , ~35–60k peak (it's fan-in) With 95% cache hit cache the user object by id origin sees ~","keywords":["scale","the","user","service","is","fan","in"]},{"url":"expert/user-management.html#ops","page":"Design a user management system","section":"Failure modes & observability","tool":"expert","text":"09 Failure modes & observability 🧱 Critical dependency If the user service is down, everything that resolves \"who is this\" degrades. Aggressive caching (serve stale on failure), circuit breakers on callers, read replicas + multi-AZ. 📉 Funnel metrics Signups started → completed → email-verification","keywords":["failure","modes","observability"]},{"url":"expert/user-management.html#recap","page":"Design a user management system","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"expert","text":"10 Trade-offs & interview recap A user invokes their GDPR right to erasure, but they have years of immutable transaction-ledger entries. What do you do? Hard-delete everything including the ledger Anonymize the PII in place and keep a non-identifying tombstone, retaining the ledger under Art. 17(3)'","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"expert/org-management.html","page":"Design organizations & teams","section":"Design organizations & teams.","tool":"expert","text":"Design organizations & teams. B2B SaaS isn't one user per account — it's a company (org/workspace) full of users, with teams, roles, and invitations. The whole thing rests on one structural insight: the membership is its own entity , a join between user and org that carries the role. Get that right ","keywords":["design","organizations","teams"]},{"url":"expert/org-management.html#requirements","page":"Design organizations & teams","section":"Requirements & the core model","tool":"expert","text":"01 Requirements & the core model ✅ Functional Create orgs; invite/remove members with roles; nested teams; seats for billing; ownership transfer; org-scoped resources that survive members leaving. 📐 Non-functional A user belongs to many orgs; an org has many users — and a user's role is per-org . M","keywords":["requirements","the","core","model"]},{"url":"expert/org-management.html#model","page":"Design organizations & teams","section":"Data model: the membership graph","tool":"expert","text":"02 Data model: the membership graph org.sql Copy CREATE TABLE organizations ( id uuid PRIMARY KEY, name text, slug text UNIQUE ); -- THE junction: one row = one user's relationship to one org. Carries role + status. CREATE TABLE memberships ( user_id uuid NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASC","keywords":["data","model","the","membership","graph","CREATE","TABLE","organizations","id","uuid","PRIMARY","KEY","name","text","slug","UNIQUE","THE","junction","one","row","user","relationship","to","org","Carries","role","status","memberships","user_id","NOT","NULL","REFERENCES","users","ON","DELETE","CASCADE","org_id","DEFAULT","member","owner"]},{"url":"expert/org-management.html#roles","page":"Design organizations & teams","section":"Roles & seats","tool":"expert","text":"03 Roles & seats The role ladder owner (full control incl. delete + transfer; ≥1 always required) · admin (manage members/settings, can't destroy) · member (default) · billing · guest (external/limited). Plus custom roles (a \"logical grouping of permissions\"; the deep model is on the authorization p","keywords":["roles","seats"]},{"url":"expert/org-management.html#hierarchy","page":"Design organizations & teams","section":"Nested teams & inheritance","tool":"expert","text":"04 Nested teams & inheritance Teams nest via parent_team_id ; permission flows down the tree — a member of a parent team implicitly belongs to all descendants for access. Resources can be owned at org, team, or user level (layered inheritance with local overrides, Notion-style): Permission inherits ","keywords":["nested","teams","inheritance"]},{"url":"expert/org-management.html#invites","page":"Design organizations & teams","section":"Invitations","tool":"expert","text":"05 Invitations The challenge: you invite an email , which may not have an account yet. So the invitation exists independently of any user, keyed by (org, email) , and resolves to a membership only on accept. Invite by email → accept → membership ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0.5× 1× 2× accept_invite.py Copy def acc","keywords":["invitations","def","accept_invite","raw_token","user","is","authenticated","by","here","inv","db","one","SELECT","FROM","WHERE","token_hash","AND","status","pending","FOR","UPDATE","sha256","row","lock","single","use","no","race","if","None","or","now","expires_at","raise","Invalid","expired","already","used","email","lower"]},{"url":"expert/org-management.html#api","page":"Design organizations & teams","section":"API & the graph queries","tool":"expert","text":"06 API & the graph queries POST /orgs/{org}/invitations · /invitations/accept invite by email; accept by token GET /me/organizations · /orgs/{org}/memberships \"my orgs\" (idx_mem_user); \"org members\" (idx_mem_org, paginate) PUT /memberships/{id} · /orgs/{org}/transfer-ownership change role (last-owne","keywords":["api","the","graph","queries"]},{"url":"expert/org-management.html#ownership","page":"Design organizations & teams","section":"Ownership transfer & last-owner protection","tool":"expert","text":"07 Ownership transfer & last-owner protection You may never delete or demote the only owner. Enforce server-side inside the same transaction, with a lock so two concurrent \"demote owner\" requests can't both pass: last_owner.sql Copy -- Inside the demote/remove transaction, before committing: SELECT ","keywords":["ownership","transfer","last","owner","protection","Inside","the","demote","remove","transaction","before","committing","SELECT","count","FROM","memberships","WHERE","org_id","org","AND","role","status","active","user_id","target","FOR","UPDATE","must","be","else","abort","keep","an"]},{"url":"expert/org-management.html#scale","page":"Design organizations & teams","section":"Scale, failure modes & observability","tool":"expert","text":"08 Scale, failure modes & observability Quantity Assumption Result Memberships 100k orgs × ~20 members ≈ 2M rows (~400 MB) — trivially indexed, never the bottleneck \"List my orgs\" bounded by orgs/user (single digits) sub-ms via (user_id, org_id) ; cache in the session \"List org members\" enterprise o","keywords":["scale","failure","modes","observability"]},{"url":"expert/org-management.html#recap","page":"Design organizations & teams","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"expert","text":"09 Trade-offs & interview recap Why model role on a memberships join table instead of putting a role column on the user? It saves a table Role is a property of the user-org relationship — the same user is owner in one org and guest in another — so it must live on the edge, which also makes every aut","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"expert/authentication.html","page":"Design authentication (login, SSO, MFA)","section":"Design authentication.","tool":"expert","text":"Design authentication. \"Who are you?\" — proving identity, then carrying that proof safely on every request. The two decisions that define the system are how you store credentials (Argon2id, never a fast hash) and how you carry the session (revocable server-side sessions vs stateless JWTs, which can'","keywords":["design","authentication"]},{"url":"expert/authentication.html#requirements","page":"Design authentication (login, SSO, MFA)","section":"Requirements & scope","tool":"expert","text":"01 Requirements & scope ✅ Functional Register/login; sessions; password reset; MFA; social login (OAuth/OIDC); enterprise SSO (SAML/OIDC) + SCIM provisioning; logout / \"log out everywhere\". 📐 Non-functional Phishing-resistant where possible; token validation on the API hot path (every request); a h","keywords":["requirements","scope"]},{"url":"expert/authentication.html#passwords","page":"Design authentication (login, SSO, MFA)","section":"Password storage: Argon2id","tool":"expert","text":"02 Password storage: Argon2id Passwords are never encrypted (reversible) — they're run through a slow, salted, memory-hard function. The 2026 OWASP default is Argon2id (min 19 MiB / 2 iterations / 1 parallelism); bcrypt (cost ≥ 10, 72-byte limit) only for legacy. Never a fast hash — GPUs do tens of ","keywords":["password","storage","argon2id","from","argon2","import","PasswordHasher","OWASP","minimum","params","ph","memory_cost","time_cost","parallelism","def","hash_password","plain","pepper","pre","hmac_sha384","key","data","app","wide","secret","in","KMS","NOT","the","DB","return","hash","PHC","string","embeds","algo","random","per","pw","SALT"]},{"url":"expert/authentication.html#sessions","page":"Design authentication (login, SSO, MFA)","section":"Sessions vs JWT, & refresh rotation","tool":"expert","text":"03 Sessions vs JWT, & refresh rotation The core trade-off is revocability vs statelessness : The standard 2026 design: 5–15 min access token + a rotating refresh token (each refresh issues a new one, single-use). If an already-used refresh token reappears, it's theft — revoke the whole token family ","keywords":["sessions","vs","jwt","refresh","rotation","def","presented","rec","store","get_refresh","hash","tokens","stored","HASHED","never","raw","if","is","None","raise","Reject","unknown","used","rotated","token","replayed","THEFT","revoke_family","family_id","nuke","the","whole","lineage","alert_security","user_id","reuse","detected","revoked","or","now"]},{"url":"expert/authentication.html#mfa","page":"Design authentication (login, SSO, MFA)","section":"MFA & passkeys","tool":"expert","text":"04 MFA & passkeys Passkeys / WebAuthn (FIDO2) The 2026 direction : a keypair bound to the origin , so a proxy phishing site can't get a usable signature — phishing-resistant by construction , defeating the AitM attack that breaks passwords/TOTP/SMS. The private key never leaves the device; the serve","keywords":["mfa","passkeys"]},{"url":"expert/authentication.html#oauth","page":"Design authentication (login, SSO, MFA)","section":"OAuth2 / OIDC","tool":"expert","text":"05 OAuth2 / OIDC OAuth 2.0 = delegated authorization ; OIDC adds an identity layer (the id_token ) for authentication . The 2026 default for all client types is the authorization code flow + PKCE (OAuth 2.1 removes implicit + password grants). PKCE binds the code to the originating client, so a stol","keywords":["oauth2","oidc"]},{"url":"expert/authentication.html#sso","page":"Design authentication (login, SSO, MFA)","section":"Enterprise SSO & SCIM","tool":"expert","text":"06 Enterprise SSO & SCIM SAML vs OIDC SAML 2.0 still dominates the enterprise install base; OIDC is preferred for new/mobile. Prefer SP-initiated flows (request-bound, replay-resistant; OIDC mandates it). SCIM Login (SAML/OIDC) only handles the moment of auth; SCIM handles lifecycle — the IdP create","keywords":["enterprise","sso","scim"]},{"url":"expert/authentication.html#capacity","page":"Design authentication (login, SSO, MFA)","section":"Capacity & token validation","tool":"expert","text":"07 Capacity & token validation Quantity Assumption (10M MAU / 1M DAU) Result Logins/s ~1M/day, peak 5× ~12/s avg, ~60/s peak — Argon2id (tens of ms) sized to the login rate Token validations/s 1M DAU × ~100 API calls ~1,160/s avg, ~6,000/s peak — the dominant number Stateless JWT validate RS256 sign","keywords":["capacity","token","validation"]},{"url":"expert/authentication.html#failure","page":"Design authentication (login, SSO, MFA)","section":"Failure modes & security","tool":"expert","text":"08 Failure modes & security 🎯 Credential stuffing The #1 vector (billions of attempts/mo). Defend with a breached-password check (HIBP k-anonymity — send the first 5 hex of the SHA-1, match the rest locally; the password never leaves you), per-account + per-IP rate limits, and MFA/passkeys. 🔓 Toke","keywords":["failure","modes","security"]},{"url":"expert/authentication.html#ops","page":"Design authentication (login, SSO, MFA)","section":"Observability, SLOs & 2026 notes","tool":"expert","text":"09 Observability, SLOs & 2026 notes Metrics & SLOs Login success/failure rate (a failure spike = stuffing in progress; segment by IP/ASN/geo), MFA + passkey adoption , token-validation latency (p99 < 10 ms), Argon2id verify latency, refresh-reuse/family-revocation events (security alerts). Auth-serv","keywords":["observability","slos","notes"]},{"url":"expert/authentication.html#recap","page":"Design authentication (login, SSO, MFA)","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"expert","text":"10 Trade-offs & interview recap You use stateless JWTs for scale. How do you handle \"log this user out everywhere right now\"? You can't — JWTs are forever Keep access tokens short-lived (5-15 min) and revoke the refresh tokens / bump a token-version; for instant kill, add the jti to a small denylist","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"expert/authorization.html","page":"Design authorization (RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC)","section":"Design authorization.","tool":"expert","text":"Design authorization. \"What can you do?\" — the decision made on every request, often many times per request, that decides whether a known principal may act on a resource. The arc of the field is RBAC → ABAC → ReBAC : from coarse roles, to attribute policies, to Google Zanzibar 's relationship graph ","keywords":["design","authorization"]},{"url":"expert/authorization.html#requirements","page":"Design authorization (RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC)","section":"Requirements & the three models","tool":"expert","text":"01 Requirements & the three models ✅ Functional check(user, action, resource) ; answer \"who can access X?\" and \"what can U access?\"; scoped per tenant/org; policy + relationship changes are audited. 📐 Non-functional On the hot path → single-digit-ms p99 even with fan-out; correct (no cross-tenant o","keywords":["requirements","the","three","models"]},{"url":"expert/authorization.html#rbac","page":"Design authorization (RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC)","section":"RBAC deep dive","tool":"expert","text":"02 RBAC deep dive Users → roles → permissions, with the role assignment scoped per org (the binding lives on the membership , not the global user): rbac.sql Copy roles(id, org_id, name) -- roles are tenant-scoped permissions(id, resource, action) -- e.g. ('document','edit') role_permissions(role_id,","keywords":["rbac","deep","dive","roles","id","org_id","name","are","tenant","scoped","permissions","resource","action","document","edit","role_permissions","role_id","permission_id","user_roles","user_id","the","user","org","role","binding","The","permission","check","flat","predicate","is","what","prevents","escalation","SELECT","EXISTS","FROM","ur","JOIN","rp"]},{"url":"expert/authorization.html#zanzibar","page":"Design authorization (RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC)","section":"Zanzibar / ReBAC: the relationship graph","tool":"expert","text":"03 Zanzibar / ReBAC: the relationship graph Zanzibar (Google) stores ACLs as relation tuples object#relation@user , e.g. doc:readme#viewer@user:anne . A userset ( @group:eng#member ) points at a set; rewrites compute relations — computed_userset (viewer ⊇ editor) and tuple_to_userset (a doc's viewer","keywords":["zanzibar","rebac","the","relationship","graph","Zanzibar","style","Check","via","walk","object","relation","user","def","check","obj","seen","if","in","return","False","cycle","guard","add","rule","config","type","rewrite","for","tuples","direct","True","is_userset","and","group","rel","eng","member","r2","computed_usersets"]},{"url":"expert/authorization.html#consistency","page":"Design authorization (RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC)","section":"Consistency: the \"new enemy\" problem","tool":"expert","text":"04 Consistency: the \"new enemy\" problem Why a global ordering matters Alice removes Bob from a doc, then adds sensitive content. If a check reads a stale ACL replica, Bob — the \"new enemy\" — sees content he should never have. Zanzibar runs on Spanner/TrueTime for external consistency and hands clien","keywords":["consistency","the","new","enemy","problem"]},{"url":"expert/authorization.html#pdp","page":"Design authorization (RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC)","section":"Policy-as-data vs policy-as-code; PEP/PDP/PIP","tool":"expert","text":"05 Policy-as-data vs policy-as-code; PEP/PDP/PIP The reference architecture: the PEP (enforcement point — your middleware/gateway) asks the PDP (decision point) \"allowed?\", which evaluates policy using data from the PIP (information point — attributes/tuples). A permission check (PEP → PDP → PIP) ⏮ ","keywords":["policy","as","data","vs","code","pep","pdp","pip"]},{"url":"expert/authorization.html#performance","page":"Design authorization (RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC)","section":"Performance: the hot path","tool":"expert","text":"06 Performance: the hot path Authz runs on every request, often many times (a list page → N object checks: the \"N+1 authz\" problem ). The budget is brutal: Quantity Assumption Result Authz QPS 50k req/s × ~8 checks/req ≈ 400k checks/s — batch the fan-out Latency budget p99 API budget ~200 ms even a ","keywords":["performance","the","hot","path"]},{"url":"expert/authorization.html#api","page":"Design authorization (RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC)","section":"API, multi-tenancy & failure modes","tool":"expert","text":"07 API, multi-tenancy & failure modes CHECK check(user, action, resource) · batch-check([…]) the hot-path decision; batch cures N+1 EXPAND expand(obj, rel) · list-objects(user, action) \"who can access X?\" / \"what can U access?\" Multi-tenancy Every tuple/role is scoped to a tenant (prefix object ids ","keywords":["api","multi","tenancy","failure","modes"]},{"url":"expert/authorization.html#ops","page":"Design authorization (RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC)","section":"Observability & 2026","tool":"expert","text":"08 Observability & 2026 Metrics Decision latency p50/p99 + batch sizes (watch N+1 fan-out); allow/deny rates (a deny spike = misconfig/attack, an allow spike = policy regression); policy/tuple change audit log (who changed what, when); decision logging (principal, action, resource, decision, policy ","keywords":["observability"]},{"url":"expert/authorization.html#recap","page":"Design authorization (RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC)","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"expert","text":"09 Trade-offs & interview recap Your product is Google-Docs-style: any user can share any document with any other, and folders grant access to the docs inside them. Which model? RBAC — one role per document ReBAC (Zanzibar-style) — per-object relationship tuples with folder→doc inheritance via tuple","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-catalog.html","page":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart","section":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart.","tool":"expert","text":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart. The read side of a store — browse, search, and the cart — is overwhelmingly read-dominated (browse-to-buy ratios of 100:1+). So it's an exercise in caching, a denormalized search read-model over an inverted index, and one subtle correctness rule: a cart doe","keywords":["design","commerce","catalog","search","cart"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-catalog.html#requirements","page":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"expert","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Browse categories, full-text + faceted search (filter by brand/size/price with counts), product detail (variants), add to cart, see prices/promotions. 📐 Non-functional Read-heavy (100:1+); fast (p99 detail < ~150 ms, search < ~300 ms); survives flash-sale spikes","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-catalog.html#estimate","page":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart","section":"Capacity estimation","tool":"expert","text":"02 Capacity estimation Drag the model (here \"writes/user/day\" = purchases, \"read:write\" = the browse:buy ratio): Tens of thousands of browse reads/s, hundreds of purchases/s. Real anchors: Algolia peaked 119,680 search QPS on Black Friday 2025; Shopify served 489M edge req/min (~8M/s) with a ~2,500:","keywords":["capacity","estimation"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-catalog.html#api","page":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart","section":"API","tool":"expert","text":"03 API GET /products/{id} · /categories/{id}/products product+variants (CDN-cacheable); PLP = search with empty q + category filter GET /search?q=&filter=brand:Nike,price:50..150&facets=brand,size → { hits, facets:{brand:{Nike:42}}, nbHits } POST /cart/items · /cart/merge add (idempotent on (cart,sk","keywords":["api"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-catalog.html#model","page":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart","section":"Data model: product vs SKU","tool":"expert","text":"04 Data model: product vs SKU Product → variant/SKU The product is the sellable concept (\"Acme Trail Runner\"); the SKU/variant is the purchasable unit (\"red, size 10\"). Product-level: title, description, category, brand, attribute schema. SKU-level: the immutable SKU code, distinguishing attributes,","keywords":["data","model","product","vs","sku"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-catalog.html#architecture","page":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart","section":"Architecture: a derived search read-model","tool":"expert","text":"05 Architecture: a derived search read-model Catalog is the system of record; search is a denormalized, async-fed read model that owns full-text, facets, and ranking — and is allowed to lag. Category browse is \"search with an empty query + a category filter.\" Browse / search read path ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 ","keywords":["architecture","derived","search","read","model"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-catalog.html#search","page":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart","section":"Search & faceted navigation","tool":"expert","text":"06 Search & faceted navigation SQL LIKE '%term%' is a leading wildcard → full table scan, hopeless at 10⁶–10⁸ products. The right tool is an inverted index (term → posting list of doc ids; queries are set intersections, sublinear and shardable). Facets are value counts over the filtered set (so user","keywords":["search","faceted","navigation","POST","products","_search","query","bool","must","multi_match","running","shoes","fields","title","brand","description","fuzziness","AUTO","filter","term","category","footwear","not","scored","Nike","CACHED","as","bitset","range","price","gte","lte","150","aggs","facet","COUNTS","over","the","filtered","set"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-catalog.html#cart","page":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart","section":"The cart (and why it doesn't reserve stock)","tool":"expert","text":"07 The cart (and why it doesn't reserve stock) Cart ≠ reservation Adding to cart does not hold inventory — almost every major platform works this way. Reserving on add-to-cart would let idle/abandoned carts lock stock indefinitely. The consequence is an oversell window : the availability check and t","keywords":["the","cart","and","why","it","doesn","reserve","stock"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-catalog.html#pricing","page":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart","section":"Pricing, promotions & read scaling","tool":"expert","text":"08 Pricing, promotions & read scaling Pricing is computed, not flat The price a user sees is a function of a price book (per currency/segment) + active promotions + loyalty tier + region/tax + time. A flat column can't express \"Nike 20% off this weekend for loyalty members in EUR\" — a rules engine e","keywords":["pricing","promotions","read","scaling"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-catalog.html#ops","page":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart","section":"Consistency, failure modes & observability","tool":"expert","text":"09 Consistency, failure modes & observability 🔁 Eventual is fine here Catalog/price propagating to the index + caches in seconds is acceptable; \"only 2 left\" is an approximate, cached nudge, not a guarantee. Strong consistency is needed exactly once — the stock decrement at checkout. 🔎 Search inde","keywords":["consistency","failure","modes","observability"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-catalog.html#recap","page":"Design e-commerce: catalog, search & cart","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"expert","text":"10 Trade-offs & interview recap Why not implement product search with SELECT … WHERE name LIKE '%term%' , and why doesn't the cart reserve inventory? LIKE is fine; carts should reserve stock A leading-wildcard LIKE can't use an index (full scan) — use an inverted index with facets/ranking; and reser","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-checkout.html","page":"Design e-commerce: checkout, inventory & orders","section":"Design e-commerce: checkout, inventory & orders.","tool":"expert","text":"Design e-commerce: checkout, inventory & orders. The write path is all about correctness : never sell the same last item twice, never double-charge a double-click, never lose a paid order. There's no distributed ACID transaction across inventory + payment + order — so correctness comes from a saga w","keywords":["design","commerce","checkout","inventory","orders"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-checkout.html#requirements","page":"Design e-commerce: checkout, inventory & orders","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"expert","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Reserve stock, take payment, create an order, hand to fulfillment, track order state. Refunds + cancellation. 📐 Non-functional Zero oversell as a hard target; exactly-once order despite retries; strong consistency where money/stock lives; survive flash-sale cont","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-checkout.html#api","page":"Design e-commerce: checkout, inventory & orders","section":"API","tool":"expert","text":"02 API POST /orders Idempotency-Key: … · cart snapshot + reservation token → 201 (or replays the prior response) POST /inventory/reservations atomic, conditional → { reservation_id, ttl } GET /orders/{id} state + history; PSP/OMS webhooks drive transitions (at-least-once → idempotent)","keywords":["api"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-checkout.html#saga","page":"Design e-commerce: checkout, inventory & orders","section":"The checkout saga","tool":"expert","text":"03 The checkout saga Checkout spans inventory + payment + order across services. No 2PC — use a saga : a sequence of local transactions, each with a compensating transaction that semantically undoes it. Orchestrate the money-critical core (a durable-execution engine like Temporal makes compensations","keywords":["the","checkout","saga"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-checkout.html#inventory","page":"Design e-commerce: checkout, inventory & orders","section":"Inventory reservation & oversell prevention","tool":"expert","text":"04 Inventory reservation & oversell prevention The race: two buyers both read qty=1 , both decide \"in stock,\" both write qty=0 → you sold 2 of 1. Read-then-write without atomicity = oversell. The strategies: reserve.sql Copy -- Conditional update: the WHERE guard makes oversell impossible; rows-affe","keywords":["inventory","reservation","oversell","prevention","Conditional","update","the","WHERE","guard","makes","impossible","rows","affected","is","signal","UPDATE","SET","available","qty","sku_id","sku","AND","never","go","negative","rows_affected","reserved","OK","OUT","OF","STOCK","do","NOT","proceed","Flash","sale","front","hot","SKU","with"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-checkout.html#order","page":"Design e-commerce: checkout, inventory & orders","section":"Order state machine + the outbox","tool":"expert","text":"05 Order state machine + the outbox The order is a guarded state machine: created → payment_pending → paid → fulfilling → shipped → delivered (+ cancelled / refunded ), every transition appending to a history table. You can't atomically \"write the order row\" and \"publish OrderCreated to Kafka\" (two ","keywords":["order","state","machine","the","outbox","BEGIN","Idempotent","create","double","click","carries","SAME","key","nd","is","no","op","INSERT","INTO","orders","id","idempotency_key","customer_id","status","total","VALUES","cust","paid","ON","CONFLICT","DO","NOTHING","Same","local","txn","enqueue","event","in","dual","write"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-checkout.html#payment","page":"Design e-commerce: checkout, inventory & orders","section":"Payment & consistency","tool":"expert","text":"06 Payment & consistency PSP call outside the DB txn Never hold a DB transaction open across a network call to Stripe/Adyen — it pins a connection and risks a DB timeout vs PSP success. Thread your idempotency key through to the PSP's key (see payments ). The \"did payment succeed?\" unknown state On ","keywords":["payment","consistency"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-checkout.html#ops","page":"Design e-commerce: checkout, inventory & orders","section":"Failure modes, multi-region & observability","tool":"expert","text":"07 Failure modes, multi-region & observability 💸 Payment ok, order write fails Outbox + saga compensation: retry the order write; if permanently failing, idempotent refund. A reconciliation job matches PSP charges to orders. ⏲️ Reserved but abandoned Reservation TTL + reaper (or Redis key expiry): ","keywords":["failure","modes","multi","region","observability"]},{"url":"expert/ecommerce-checkout.html#recap","page":"Design e-commerce: checkout, inventory & orders","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"expert","text":"08 Trade-offs & interview recap Two shoppers race to buy the last unit. How do you guarantee you don't oversell — without serializing every buyer of that SKU? SELECT the quantity, check it in app code, then UPDATE An atomic conditional update — UPDATE … SET available=available-n WHERE available >= n","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"expert/billing.html","page":"Design billing, subscriptions & metering","section":"Design billing, subscriptions & metering.","tool":"expert","text":"Design billing, subscriptions & metering. The layer that decides how much to charge and when — plans, subscriptions, and (increasingly) usage-based metering — sitting above the payment system that moves the money. It's a correctness problem, not a throughput one: metering is high-volume (billions of","keywords":["design","billing","subscriptions","metering"]},{"url":"expert/billing.html#requirements","page":"Design billing, subscriptions & metering","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"expert","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Plans/prices; subscriptions (seats); usage metering (ingest → aggregate → rate); invoicing with proration + tax; dunning on failed payments. 📐 Non-functional Idempotent everywhere (a double-counted event = overcharge); reproducible/auditable rating; the billing ","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"expert/billing.html#api","page":"Design billing, subscriptions & metering","section":"API & data model","tool":"expert","text":"02 API & data model POST /v1/usage { event_name, customer_id, value, ts, identifier } → 202 (async; dedup on identifier) POST /v1/subscriptions · PUT /v1/subscriptions/{id} create; change plan with proration_behavior GET /v1/invoices/{id} lines[], subtotal, tax, total, amount_due Price book products","keywords":["api","data","model"]},{"url":"expert/billing.html#architecture","page":"Design billing, subscriptions & metering","section":"Architecture: ingest → aggregate → rate → invoice","tool":"expert","text":"03 Architecture: ingest → aggregate → rate → invoice Metering is async/eventually-consistent (events trickle in, late events happen); invoicing is a transactional point-in-time snapshot that freezes usage at cycle close. Getting that freeze boundary right is the crux. Usage → invoice ⏮ ‹ ▶ › 1 / 1 0","keywords":["architecture","ingest","aggregate","rate","invoice"]},{"url":"expert/billing.html#metering","page":"Design billing, subscriptions & metering","section":"Usage metering: ingest → aggregate → rate","tool":"expert","text":"04 Usage metering: ingest → aggregate → rate Each stage has a correctness trap. Ingest must be idempotent — at-least-once delivery is a given, so every event carries a client-generated identifier and the store enforces uniqueness (double-counting overcharges the customer — the cardinal sin). The met","keywords":["usage","metering","ingest","aggregate","rate","Idempotent","ON","CONFLICT","makes","duplicate","event_id","no","op","double","count","INSERT","INTO","usage_events","meter","customer_id","value","ts","VALUES","DO","NOTHING","dedup","is","the","whole","game","Pre","per","customer","period","into","compact","buckets","so","invoicing","reads"]},{"url":"expert/billing.html#pricing","page":"Design billing, subscriptions & metering","section":"Pricing models","tool":"expert","text":"05 Pricing models The volume-vs-graduated distinction is a classic interview test: same 6 units, same tiers, but $39 (volume) vs $41.50 (graduated) . Keep rating pure and reproducible — same aggregate + same price version → the same amount forever (needed for audit/recompute).","keywords":["pricing","models"]},{"url":"expert/billing.html#invoicing","page":"Design billing, subscriptions & metering","section":"Invoicing, proration & dunning","tool":"expert","text":"06 Invoicing, proration & dunning Invoice run At each cycle boundary: recurring items + frozen rated usage + prorations + credits → subtotal → tax (Stripe Tax / Avalara, computed at finalize by geo) → total → draft → finalize → charge. Draft/finalize separation lets late usage land before the invoic","keywords":["invoicing","proration","dunning"]},{"url":"expert/billing.html#ops","page":"Design billing, subscriptions & metering","section":"Correctness, observability & 2026","tool":"expert","text":"07 Correctness, observability & 2026 Ledger & RevRec The double-entry ledger is the source of truth — invoices/charges/refunds/credits post balanced entries; balances are derived. Billing ≠ revenue : recognize revenue when the obligation is satisfied (ASC 606) — usage as consumed, annual upfront rat","keywords":["correctness","observability"]},{"url":"expert/billing.html#recap","page":"Design billing, subscriptions & metering","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"expert","text":"08 Trade-offs & interview recap Your metering pipeline receives usage events at-least-once (clients retry). Why is the event's identifier the most important field? It sorts the events It's the idempotency key — dedup on it (INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING) so a retried/duplicate event isn't counted ","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"expert/audit-log.html","page":"Design an audit log / activity feed","section":"Design an audit log / activity feed.","tool":"expert","text":"Design an audit log / activity feed. Two systems that share an ingestion path but diverge hard on guarantees. A compliance audit log optimizes for durability, immutability, tamper-evidence, and multi-year retention — every privileged action, provable to an auditor. A product activity feed (\"Sarah co","keywords":["design","an","audit","log","activity","feed"]},{"url":"expert/audit-log.html#requirements","page":"Design an audit log / activity feed","section":"Requirements & scale","tool":"expert","text":"01 Requirements & scale ✅ Functional Append an event (who/what/when/where/outcome); query by actor/resource/time for compliance; export for auditors; serve a per-user/per-resource activity feed. 📐 Non-functional Never lose an event (durability is paramount); tamper-evident (an admin can't quietly e","keywords":["requirements","scale"]},{"url":"expert/audit-log.html#api","page":"Design an audit log / activity feed","section":"API & data model","tool":"expert","text":"02 API & data model POST /v1/events Idempotency-Key: … → { id, seq, hash } GET /v1/events?actor=&resource=&from=&to=&tenant= compliance query (cursor-paginated, tenant-scoped) GET /v1/verify?from_seq=&to_seq= · /v1/feeds/{user} chain consistency proof; activity-feed read The event is structured and ","keywords":["api","data","model"]},{"url":"expert/audit-log.html#ingestion","page":"Design an audit log / activity feed","section":"Architecture & ingestion","tool":"expert","text":"03 Architecture & ingestion Don't lose events, don't block the business transaction, don't 2PC across DB + Kafka. The answer is the transactional outbox : write the audit row in the same DB transaction as the business change; a CDC relay ships it onward. The immutable store is the system of record; ","keywords":["architecture","ingestion"]},{"url":"expert/audit-log.html#tamper","page":"Design an audit log / activity feed","section":"Tamper-evidence: hash chaining","tool":"expert","text":"04 Tamper-evidence: hash chaining Each entry binds the previous one: entry.hash = H(prev_hash ‖ payload) . Edit any entry and its hash changes — which breaks every entry after it. You can't silently alter the middle (exactly how AWS CloudTrail's chained, signed digest files and QLDB's journal worked","keywords":["tamper","evidence","hash","chaining","import","hashlib","json","GENESIS","def","entry_hash","prev_hash","payload","sha256","update","bytes","fromhex","bind","the","predecessor","dumps","sort_keys","True","DETERMINISTIC","serialization","separators","encode","or","hashes","won","reproduce","return","hexdigest","append","log","prev","if","else","seq","len","never"]},{"url":"expert/audit-log.html#worm","page":"Design an audit log / activity feed","section":"Anchoring, WORM & retention","tool":"expert","text":"05 Anchoring, WORM & retention Anchoring / notarization Hash chaining alone is defeatable by an operator who rewrites the whole chain — so publish the current Merkle root (a signed checkpoint) to an external witness on a cadence (a second account, a customer bucket, a transparency log). Once witness","keywords":["anchoring","worm","retention"]},{"url":"expert/audit-log.html#query","page":"Design an audit log / activity feed","section":"Query, the feed & consistency","tool":"expert","text":"06 Query, the feed & consistency 🔎 Compliance search Filter by actor/resource/action/time/outcome, tenant-scoped (mandatory tenant_id filter — cross-tenant leakage in a shared log is the catastrophic bug). The compliance log never fans out — it's query-on-read. 📰 Activity feed This is the news-fee","keywords":["query","the","feed","consistency"]},{"url":"expert/audit-log.html#ops","page":"Design an audit log / activity feed","section":"Failure modes, observability & 2026","tool":"expert","text":"07 Failure modes, observability & 2026 Failure modes Dropped events / chain gaps (seq-gap alert = Sev incident; outbox replay); ingestion lag (autoscale, backpressure — never drop); chain-verification failure (freeze writes, forensic review — a potential breach); legal-hold/retention violation (alar","keywords":["failure","modes","observability"]},{"url":"expert/audit-log.html#recap","page":"Design an audit log / activity feed","section":"Trade-offs & interview recap","tool":"expert","text":"08 Trade-offs & interview recap An admin with database access quietly edits an old audit entry to hide an action. How does the system make that detectable? Restrict database permissions Hash-chain entries (each stores hash(prev_hash + payload)) so editing one breaks every later entry's hash, plus WO","keywords":["trade","offs","interview","recap"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html","page":"Glossary","section":"Glossary.","tool":"reference","text":"Glossary. Every term used in this guide, defined in one line. Skim it before an interview, or jump here whenever a word on another page is fuzzy. Grouped by theme; press / to search the whole guide.","keywords":["glossary"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#fundamentals","page":"Glossary","section":"Fundamentals","tool":"reference","text":"Fundamentals Latency Time for one request to complete. You optimise the tail (p99), not just the average. Percentile (p50 / p95 / p99) The latency value a fraction of requests beat: p99 = 99% are faster, the slowest 1% worse (p50 = median; p999 = p99.9). Optimise the tail — fan-out makes a rare slow","keywords":["fundamentals"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#networking","page":"Glossary","section":"Networking & delivery","tool":"reference","text":"Networking & delivery Load balancer Spreads requests across a server pool and routes around dead ones (L4 = transport, L7 = HTTP-aware). Reverse proxy Server-side proxy facing clients; basis of an API gateway. API gateway The front door: TLS, auth, rate limiting, and routing in one place. CDN Global","keywords":["networking","delivery"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#data","page":"Glossary","section":"Data & storage","tool":"reference","text":"Data & storage ACID The strong transaction guarantees of relational databases: A tomicity (all-or-nothing), C onsistency (a valid state moves only to another valid state), I solation (concurrent transactions don't see each other's half-done work), D urability (once committed, data survives a crash).","keywords":["data","storage"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#caching-messaging","page":"Glossary","section":"Caching & messaging","tool":"reference","text":"Caching & messaging Cache-aside App reads cache, falls back to the DB on a miss and fills the cache. The default pattern. Write-through / write-back Write to cache+DB together / write to cache and flush to DB later (fast but loss-prone). Eviction (LRU / LFU) Policies for dropping data when the cache","keywords":["caching","messaging"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#reliability","page":"Glossary","section":"Reliability & consistency","tool":"reference","text":"Reliability & consistency CAP theorem During a partition, choose Consistency or Availability (Partition tolerance is mandatory). PACELC CAP plus the normal case: Else, trade Latency vs Consistency. The 2026-expected framing. Strong consistency Every read sees the latest write. Needs coordination → c","keywords":["reliability","consistency"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html","page":"The cheat sheet","section":"The cheat sheet.","tool":"reference","text":"The cheat sheet. One page to skim the night before an interview: the numbers, the formulas, the seven-step method, a few hard-won heuristics, and where to go deeper. Everything here is taught in full elsewhere in the guide — this is the compressed version.","keywords":["the","cheat","sheet"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#latency","page":"The cheat sheet","section":"Latency numbers (rounded)","tool":"reference","text":"Latency numbers (rounded) Operation Time L1 / RAM reference ~1 ns / ~100 ns Read 1 MB from RAM ~3 µs SSD random read ~16 µs Datacenter round trip ~0.5 ms Read 1 MB from SSD ~1 ms Round trip, same continent ~30 ms Round trip, intercontinental ~150 ms The story: memory ≫ SSD ≫ disk ≫ network. Cache to","keywords":["latency","numbers","rounded"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#sizes","page":"The cheat sheet","section":"Sizes & powers of two","tool":"reference","text":"Sizes & powers of two Seconds / day 86,400 ≈ 10⁵ . So 1M/day ≈ ~12/s; 1B/day ≈ ~10,000/s. 2¹⁰ / 2²⁰ / 2³⁰ / 2⁴⁰ ≈ thousand (KB) / million (MB) / billion (GB) / trillion (TB). Item sizes char 1 B · int/long 4–8 B · UUID 16 B · tweet ~300 B · photo 0.5–5 MB · 1 min HD video ~50 MB. Peak factor Multipl","keywords":["sizes","powers","of","two"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#formulas","page":"The cheat sheet","section":"Capacity formulas","tool":"reference","text":"Capacity formulas Write QPS DAU × writes-per-user-per-day ÷ 86,400 Read QPS write QPS × read:write ratio Peak QPS (read + write QPS) × peak factor Storage writes/day × bytes/item × retention-days × replication Bandwidth QPS × bytes/item Servers ⌈ peak QPS ÷ per-server QPS ⌉ Practice these on the int","keywords":["capacity","formulas"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#method","page":"The cheat sheet","section":"The 7-step method","tool":"reference","text":"The 7-step method 1 Clarify & scope Ask questions; agree a small core; name what's out of scope. 2 Requirements Functional + non-functional. \"Read-heavy or write-heavy? How fresh must data be?\" 3 Estimate QPS, storage, bandwidth — numbers justify every choice. 4 API A few endpoints to pin the contra","keywords":["the","step","method"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#heuristics","page":"The cheat sheet","section":"Heuristics that usually apply","tool":"reference","text":"Heuristics that usually apply Read-heavy? Cache hard; add read replicas; precompute. Write-heavy? Queue + batch; wide-column store; shard by the write key. Needs to feel instant globally? CDN + regional replicas; move data near users. Must be correct (money, inventory)? Strong consistency, ACID, ide","keywords":["heuristics","that","usually","apply"]},{"url":"reference/cheatsheet.html#reading","page":"The cheat sheet","section":"Further reading (verified Jun 2026)","tool":"reference","text":"Further reading (verified Jun 2026) ByteByteGo Alex Xu's visual course + System Design Interview Vol 1 & 2 (and the 2026 GenAI / ML system-design books). The best visual starting point. donnemartin/system-design-primer Free, comprehensive GitHub reference with Anki flashcards. Hello Interview — Syst","keywords":["further","reading","verified","jun"]}];
